      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <p>I</p>
        <p>mcDoUGALL's Patetit SELF-RAISING FLOUR</p>
        <p>••.•</p>
        <p>PREVENTS The Examiner, Saturday, December 22, 1906 INDIGESTION.</p>
        <p>me a beautiful day. I'm glad of that—women think so much of sunshine on their wedding- day." "That's natural enough," Vernon Salisbury returned. "There's a superstition, isn't there, that the bride on whom the sun „shines will be happy, and vice versa. I'm glad, for my own sake, that your bride will be happy; it means that I shall be free, and you will not be found out." "You free?" John Brandon interrogated. Vernon Salisbury nodded. ' "Yes, of course. I shall keep silent only so long as lam free. I--" He paused suddenly in his speech, but the careless, swinging movement of, his walk did not alter in the least, though it well might have. Coming towards them were Chief Warder Clark and Warder Sullivan. "It's unfortunate," Vernon Salisbury said in a perfectly level voice, "but you see who is coming; they won't recognise me, it's almost impossible they should.. You must stop and speak to them ; you 'must ask them if they've caught the poor fellow—that's the natural thing to do' and you must do it. Don't be frightened —not a pulse in my body has quickened. Don't give yourself away—be sure that I shall not; and remember, if they take Me, there will at all events, be a postponement of., your wedding." John Brandon nodded; he 'was quite as cool ELP his companion. In bringing his thoughts back to his marriage, he had brought them to the supreme interest of his life; nothing else seemed materially the matter. "It shan't be postponed," he said, and then' the four men were within speaking _distance.</p>
        <p>Tymein tile enurch came in tor !teen scrutiny. The bride was looking beautiful indeed! Early on the night preceding her marriage it had aqmost seemed that restlessness and inability to sleep would rob her of something of her beauty and youth on the morrow. The strange notion that Dick Brandon had come back from the dead to protest against her marriage had. dis- finiAsted her; but after a time, she did not know quite what time, this feeling had passed off. seemed as though he had suddenly been wiped out; thought of him ceased to haunt her, and she slept peacefully, happily.</p>
        <p>Emily Miller, on the other hand, had turned and tossed all through the night; she believed she had seen a ghost, and the belief terrified her. It seemed to her so very, very terrible that the dead man could not rest because the woman he had wanted was about to give herself to another. It was pitiably sad! She had wept a little through the night. in a way she was glad there was to be a wedding, because a marriage is always very, very dear to a woman's heart. She may have read of hundreds of unhappy and disastrous Marriages, and yet each one is always a romance to her.</p>
        <p>More than once, as she dressed her young mistress, the temptation had come to Emily to tell what she had seen, but she had conquered it. If the dead man could not rest, there was no need the living woman should know of it. It could not, in any way, contribute to her happiness. In all probability, eyes or no eyes looking en, every bridegroom kisses his bride as they drive away from church. John Brandon caught Celia to him quite roughly—diJerently from how he had ever done before--and kissed her half-open, questioning lips, then he held her closely, smiling into her eyes.</p>
        <p>"Still on the warpath ? John—Brandon said. "I'm afraid that means you have not met with success. 'You look tired."</p>
        <p>Chief Warder Clark almost frowned. "I never was so tired in all my life," he said. "I feel as though all the mists and cold of the night have taken up their abode in my limbs, on my chest. No, we've not found him, though we've 'searched and searcbed,,and yet, I'm morally certain he's not gone far. He wouldn't think of breaking cover, so to speak, until he'd g6l rid of his Government dress, and he hasn't done that, Um pretty certain ; we'd have turned it up some- where if he had. You're up early, Mr. Brandon ? " John Brandon laughed. "You disturbed my night's rest," he said. "And I found it im- possible to woo the drowsy god again, so I got up, and made my friend, Mr. Vernon Salisbury" —he looked at the convict—"get up too. He's come down for the wedding, and seems to think we mix up things rather strangely in these parts. He doesn't understand that the escape of the prisoner was an extra, so to speak." Vernon Salisbury smiled, but did not speak. Chief Warder Clark •did not look at him. Warder Sullivan, however, seemed more inquisi- tive as to who and what he was. _ _ .</p>
        <p>-"You must forgive me," he said; "it is the first time I have kissed my wife. Dearest, you have never looked so beautiful as you look now. IR it because it is our wedding-day?" She had caught her breath, looked half frightened for a moment, then she smiled. "I'm glad .I,looked pretty," she said, quietly, "there were such a lot of people in the church. I never ey.2eeted so many, and yet I don't know why not.r'r erhaps I had an idea sentimental font would not approve df my letting one brother slip ' into the other brother's shoes. But I need not have given the matter a thought; people, it seems, are o.nly too glad to see a girl married at any price. I suppose it is the end and aim et most things feminine—l wonder why?" His clasp on her relaxed for an instant, then tightened again., "Don't talk like that!" he said, an almost rough tone in his voice. "It hurts me, Celia. Are you not a little glad you looked so pretty because I was to be your husband to-day?" For some reason or other, perhaps at the word. husband, she flushed. Bride and bridegroom seemed but vague terms—husband and wife yery definite., "Why, of course," she answered, "I am. as vain Jack, as the r est of the world of women; I like every man to `think me pretty, desirable. But in the early hour of last night it seemed to me in all probability I should be a wreck to-day. I kept "—she dropped her voice—"thinking end thinking of Dick; it seemed to me he was near me, cowing to claim me, and then—the sensation was the strangest I ever experienced--suddenly a blow seemed to fall upon me, to hurt no horribly. I elied out, but no one heard. I think the thought of this imaginary blow stunned me— fancy, of course, for it was all fancy—then, when I came to again, all the imagining that Dick was, near nie was gone. I think it was a relief. I When a man is dead it is only painful to feel his presence—and he is dead, poor fellow!" She sighed a little. The carriage was nearing; the house now. "He is dead indeed," Jack said. "Put him out of your mind, your heart, your soul ; let me only occupy it; remember you are mine--bono of my bone, flesh of my flesh; you have given yourself to me to-day, forsaking all others; you will keep yourself wholly unto me in thought, as well as in deed—that is NO, Celia ?" Ile spoke swiftly, passionately. She had repeated the marriage service like a parrot;' there was not a word or a sentence that she had analysed; the very plain speaking of the AposPe Paul and other exhortations of the ceremony had fallen upon .deaf—or at all events indifferent— . . _</p>
        <p>"I'd give my head almost to find him," Warder Clark said. "He sets us, as it were, at defiance, getting off like this, and he'll laugh, he'll laugh fit to kill hill-Bell, to think how he's done us. I know him, 'he's the sort of man that ought to have been hanged: imprisonment is useless. And yet my sympathy, I believe, is with him to a certain extent. Heaven only knows why! He must have found the night as unpleasant as I have."</p>
        <p>IN-rarder Sullivan laughed. "Oh, I don't know," he, answered. "I don't think I've minded ie discomfort much. As for sympathising, if the poor wretch is clever enough to get away with every man's hand against him, I don't grudge him his freedom. But I don't know that he will get away. In my opinion, he's likely to be over daring, too audacious. He'll, likely as not, after he's golf some different togs, stop about here, and be caught like a rat in a trap. I prognosticate we'll have him back in Dartmoor within the week."</p>
        <p>"1 hope so, with all my heart!" Chief Warder Clark said. "It's a reflection on us if prisoners escape, though it's none of our helping, you may be sure. Well, we, must be getting.OD, I suppose. We've searched every inch of the ditch, where I thought he might be hiding. The only place is the trees now, if he is to be found anywhere about here. Good-morning, Mr. Brandon, and much happiness to you." John Brandon nodded. Some of the man7s last ..vords rabg in his brain. " We've searched every wit of the ditch." How then was it they had .not come upon the body of Dick Brandon? It must have been that, as fate willed it, they had not searched every inch. "That's the explanation, of course," Vernon Salisbury said, reading the other's "They missed a little bit, and there yi lay hidden. Toil went ,through that ordeal ... ,well 7-so well, that I think you did not realise, quite how vital it was to you. I wonder what Warder Sullivan is returning for?" The under-warder was coming back at a brisk trot. As he drew nearer they saw that his face was illuminated by the broadest of broad smiles; when he reached them' he was chuckling quietly. "I could have the best laugh in the world of Warder Clark if I chose," he said. "What do you think, Mr. Brandon? He swears you hid a lady in the safe last night. We know better, don't we ? I've had to forego the best joke of my life, for your sake. It's very good, No. 27" —he turned to the convict—"but it's not good enough for me. However, I'm not going to blow the gaff. You're safe as far As I'm concerned —only, gentlemen, I 'shall expect you to see I don't lose by it." John Brandon turned pale. After the first slight shock Vernon Salisbury shewed no,sign, if any emotion. "You shan't, lose by it," Vernon Salisbury said, "you may be quite sure of that. I may take it, then, so far as you're concerned, a certain individual, who shall be nameless, is safe ? " "Quito safe, but I'd advise you not to stop about; here, and, if you do. don't talk to the prison officials. We always called you Gentleman , Jim, in a place that shall be nameless, because of your aristocratic drawl—you haven't dropped ' it, and. it's dangerous." He turned away as he spoke, laughing to himself. "Of all the cool craattires I ever came across," he'said, "commend me to him. Didn't move a hair—might have been is gentleman at large all his life. I wonder what Mr. Brandon is to him ? Only it friend. I suppose. Does he know what dangerous ground he's treading on ? So that was the lady ! If I can withstand the tempta- tion of having the laugh of Clark, I can keep a secret better than any man in the world!" For a moment or two there was silence between John' Brandon and Vernon Salisbury. Then John Brandon spoke. "You must get away," he said. "Already you are recognised." Vernon Salisbury made a careless gesture with his hand. -</p>
        <p>"Oh, that's all right," he said. "Sullivan alway 4 was one of the hest. You'll pay him, and he'll keep his mouth shut. I'm ,safer here than anywhere else, for the moment. Funny about the ;fi.-f e. isn't it And strange they've found nothing in the ditch—shews how anything hut thoroughly they search after all, doesn't it ? Well, well, :lack, dear old chap, however long we live we're never likely to ,have • narrower squeak than we had to- day, I take it I" .CHA pTpTil—xiv. •</p>
        <p>"inn afiiiid you admire this man more than do," he answered. "His presence here is a great embarrassment to- me; but there are circum- stances, into which I need not enter, which induce me to hide him. I will tell him what you say, and put his hand in his waistcoat pocket—"here's a small note for you. When I come back you shall, of course, have more. I will give you a lump sum down, which I hope will satisfy you. I don't want anything in the nature of hlttetunall hangtag over the all na. life."</p>
