      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>;MOH AND THE FREE CHURCHES</head>
        <p>;MOH AND THE FREE CHURCHES</p>
        <p>Reply By Mr. E J. Guest. At a sale of work held last Friday in the vestry of , the Wesleyan Church, Stockton Heath, Mr. E. J. Guest, J.P., (the chairman) said that he was pleased to be present, because he rejoiced in the work they were doing, and sympathised with them in bearing the burden that it necessarily involved. But all this was in vain, if, what the Rector of Warrington was reported to have said recently, was correct :—That one of the reasons why the Free Churches were wrong was that they over- lapped the work being done by the Anglican Church. By this he understood the Rector to mean that the Free Churches interfered with the progressive work of the Anglican Church. But surely the Rector was wrong, for the very opposite was the case. Speaking for the moment of the Wesleyan Church alone, was it not true that she had helped to build up the Anglican Church? Was it not the fact that Methodism had supplied a con- tinuous stream of clergymen and laymen who had proved themselves to be amongst the best workers in the Anglican Church? In this way she had not overlapped, but had been greatly helpful to the building up of the Church of Christ and to the spreading of the Grospel.Then she had acted as a stimulus and bad provoked unto love and good works. He could not understand the demand, which the Rector and others made for Uniformity. The God we worship, is not a God of Uniformity; but of infinite variety. Variety in all His works, why not variety in His Church? 'There are diversities of workings but the same God who workath all in all." I Cor..xii.. G (R.V.) The Anglican Church provided a service which met the needs of a certain section of the community and thy had not a word to say against the beauty and order of such service. But the simpler and more spontaneous service of the Free Churches, had a power to swat' the multitude. That the more stereotyped service of the Anglican Church could not effect and many of the mere zealous of the Clergy have found this out and have adopted similar services wherever they could do so. instead of overlapping the Anglican Church, the Free Churches had been distinctly helpful, not only in feeding ker ; but in presenting object lessons for her imitation. And so, the Wesleyan Church at Stockton Heath had not been erected in any spirit of antagonism or hindrance and there was no room for objection; but rather for thankfulness for the increased opportunities thus afforded for the further- ance of the Gospel. The various branches of the one tree Church were, or ought to be mutually helpful, as members of the Body of Christ, as evangelists, pastors and teachers for the perfecting of the saints, u.to the work of ministering, nnto the building up of that Body. The above is the gist of what Mr. Guest said, and to this he would add the followirig:—Tn further refutation ofjthe Rector's objection to the Free Churches, on the ground that they overlap the work •of tee Anglican Church, it may be stated as a matter of experience, that where the Church of England has the field to herself, there not infrequently is to be found a dull monotonous religious life ; but where she has the healthy rivalry, of the Free Churches there she wakes up to more vigor- ous life. Imagination alone can picture what the state of the church would have been throughout the country, had the Free Churches never come into being. We might still have had amongst us the fox hunting parson droning through the service and meeting his parishion- ers at the wayside tavern at its conclusion. No one has ever proved, to the satisfaction of the ordinary mind, that God requires all men to use the same prayer book, to adhere to the same rubrics, and to accept as essential to the beineof the church three orders in the Ministry, " Bishops,Priests and Deacons." Oar Saviour spoke of one Flock,but never of one Fold. "Other sheep I have, which are not of this Fold" see John x. IG. (R.V.) clearly indicating the existence of more than ono Fold. The essentials of true worship are to be found in His eveememorable words" God is a Spirit, and they that worship Him must worship in Spirit and Truth.' The Fold is of minor importance, the wellbeing of the sheep, is the vital thing. It is the business of the shepherds to feed and to guard the sheep, and not to waste time in trying to get them all into one Fold. When it is remembered, that in all the churches in the land,there is not accommodation for half the people,and that very much less than half the population, have apparently any desire to frequent the House of God ; it seems futile to speak of the churches overlapping one another. With so many millions of the inhabitants of this country, still unattached to any church, surely there is material enough to work upon to tax the zeal and energy of all, who wish to see the Kingdom of God extended. The rivalry of the churches should be a "holy "rivalry. We cannot all think alike audit is not designed that we should do so. Uniformity is not the watchword, but rather unity. The Unity of the Spirit, the Bond of Peace and Righteousness of Life. Men of the world are looking on the unseemly strife, which too frequently exists between the various parties existing in the Anglican Church and between the Anglican and the Free Churches. Do we wonder that they stand outside amazed. May the time soon come when all this is ended and the inAmbers of Christ's Kingdom unite their forces in love against the common foe. Then will the outside world be attracted and recruits will be more rapidly enlisted to serve in the great army of.the King.</p>
        <p>CORONER BRIGHOUSr.'S HUMOUR. Mr. Brighouse, coroner for South-West Lancashire, recently sent by train a bottle of calves'-footjelly,which arrived in a very dilapidated com-ntion at its destination, Later he sent another parcel from Liverpool to Ormskirk bearing the following label :—" Instructions to the railway company.—This parcel contains calves'- foot jelly. When it is in the van place a heavy wsight upon it. It improves the jelly. When it gets to Ormskirk, throw it violently on the platform. Broken glass mixed with the jelly improves it. Use as much violence as possible." The parcel arrived without any sign of damage.l ,</p>
        <p>COMMERCIAL FA-MUM:l.—According to " Kemp, s Mercantile Gazette," the total number of Commercial' failures recorded in England and Wales during the week ending Friday, December 14th,was 150, viz. New Bankruptcy Proceedings published in the London Gazette, 82 ; Deeds of Arrangement registered,. The respective numbers in the corresponding week of last year were : Bankruptcies, 92 ; Deeds of Arrangement, 77—total 169; being a, decrease of 19. The total num- ber of Commercid Failures recorded during the 50 weeks of the present year is 8.07 l ; the total number recorded in the corresponding 50 Weeks of last year was 5,(97, showing a decrease of 6th.</p>
