        <p>The man again looked offended, but he too!' the five-pound note held out to him. "I'm sorry you look on it in that light, sir ' he said, quite civilly. "It's nothing of the sort. I assure you. Even if you refused to give me penny I shouldn't round on Gentleman Jim; is you thought a minute you'd see that I couldn't , I'm endangering my own liberty in helping hiro to escape. When I put this note in my pocket - -Ale suited the action to the words—"l makr myself As guilty as you are. Anything you gir: me I shall look upon as a gratuity, I assure you. You'll not forget my warning? It's the man'. eyes which will give him away, if ever ha' re-taken. I'd advise him to wear blue glasses:. only that raises suspicion, at once. Well, good, - morning to you, sir, and I wish You joy. Er- only one regret in the tiff*, sir "—he began to laugh quietly—"that I cati't tell Chief Wards"- Clark who the lady in the safe was! Ho was so sure. It was a joke, wasn't it, Mr, Brandon ?"</p>
        <p>"A tremendous joke," John Brandon returned and as the prison Warder went from the reolz the happy bridegroom groaned.</p>
