ckton Heath. Miss Roscoe will enter "You have lost no timaN he said; "you have upon her duties in January and her selection will not let grass grow under your feet. How much prove very popular. The Society is receiving exten- do you want ? ' save support in the district and the Hon. Treasure" The man looked hurt, a deep flurt swept his Mr. J.. W. Bouchier, will be able to report a substantial, face; for a moment he was angry. balance when the work! is actually commenced. "I didn't come for money," he said, "though later on perhaps sir. I wouldn't come on a day like this for that. I don't know why you're harbouring the man, so respected as you have always been; but that you have, seems to me to point to something—some good in him. The good in a man never yet came out in a convict prison ; the brute, the beast—yes, the utterly callous individuality—the garbage. I might almost say. I've a sort of respect for No. 27, because he got away, and because, when be was with us, he was alway's law-abiding, did his work well, kept his cell like a new pin. And so I've come to warn you the searell'after him is very hot; known he hasn't got far. He's not tramped any of the roads ; he's not left any of the stations---' that means that they'll search and search within a twelve-mile radius of the prison, and his dis- guise isn't as good as he thinks it. I don't know whether you've noticed it, but lie's ot particularly handsome eyes, with wonderfully beautiful lashes—the eyes and the saucy tone betrayed him to me; those eyes will betray him to others. It's dead against what I ought to do, ,sir, but I've half ti mind to give you a hint." John Brandon laughed almost hysterically. What a wedding-day ! "Make it a whole mind," he said, "and give the hint." "Then tell him—it'll be a sacrifice, I know, to a vain man like him— oh !he was vain and a dandy even in prison—but tell him to cut off those long lashes ; his oyes will look lighter, harder, not no large without them. lie meedn cut them off altogether, but they curl up and almost touch his eyebrows--anything like that stamps a man ; gives him away at once. If Chief Warder Clark wasn't the greatest fool in the universe, in spite of—:-perhaps because of--- -what he calls his second-sight ; if he didn't sillier worse than anyone I've ever known from swollen head, he'd have. recognised the man just as I did. Wasn't his coolneae beautiful, sir? There's not one man in a hundred that wouldn't have run away when he saw us coming !" The admiration of the warder did not extend to :John Brandon. . This man, this escaped convict, -was the cause of all his trouble. Had he not escaped from prison, the body of his dead brother would 'still have been shut up in the safe. Had he so chosen, he need mover have ; opened it again. No one would have knovra Dick us</p>
