      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>WEDDING.</head>
        <p>THE WEDDING.</p>
        <p>One society wedding is very much like another. To all outward appearance that of John Brandon , and Celia Verde differed in no wise from number- less others. The bride was beautiful, shy, and nervous ; the .bridegroom handsome, mvkward, 14 evidently triumphant. The bridesmaids were urbtty, and mostly had the same thought in their minds—how long would it be before they stood a similar position to Celia 'Verde ? The groomsmen were mostly bored. And the eompady ,feminine made remarks about the toilets worn by others. In one respect only did this wedding differ from many others. More than dozen detectives , mingled with the company. , Knowing the ,imputhinee Qf the man, it seemed to them Gentleman Sim, it he had managed to procure other clothes, -would be more than likely to -Miter the church, to leave it witlr the crowd, and get away by train to some neighbouring village with Some who, as ordinary spectators, had just conic over to see time wedding. It would not be beyond his audacity to get into conversation with some- one or other, to accompany them to their home, even to bounce himself into it. They did not look tummy the, •-•-,-ty hot the rest cl</p>
