        <p>COMPULSORY MILITARY TRAINING.</p>
        <p>National Service League Meeting at Warrington.</p>
        <p>The compulsory military training proposals of the National Service League were advocated at a public meeting held at Brick-street Mission on Tuesday even- ing. Councillor J. .L Nield presided over a small attendance, and addresses on the objects of the League, viewed specially from the workingman's standpoint, were delivered by Mr. G. Holman Kingdon and Mr- Eustace Holden (hon. secretary of the Bolton Branch). The CHAIRMAN said that As they approached that season of tiler year when they were especially reminded of that great Advocate of peace ant' goodwill towards mankind, it seemed a little strange that they should be</p>
        <p>brought 'together to discus proble-ms of war i to discuss ways and means of meetinr, the enemy at the gate. They were entitled to ask whether the tin3e was not now draw-</p>
        <p>ing near when the nations would find some other means of settling their differences than by the arbitra- ment of the sword. As the lovers of peace they would all .feel pleased that there were .evidences of a less . . .</p>
        <p>bellicose. attitude on the part of the nations towards each other. Having compared our resources with those of Germany, he contended that although we had. a great start of that nation in the first line of defence, there was a case to be made .out for the remodelling of</p>
