        <p>• Brewery Band Concert. There was a crowded attendance at the Parr Hall on Monday evening on the occasion of the annual concert in connection with Messrs Walker's Brewery ,Band. An excellent programme had been arranged and the proceedings were in every way highly success- ful. The openingitems "Vanquisher" (Southweil) and "Don Quixote" (Bound) were admirably rendered by the band, under the conduciorship of Mr. P. J. Brady. The instrumentalists later in ihe evening contributed other selections with equal success their efforts being greatly appreciated. Mr. Alfred Brown (Manchester Gentlemen's Glee Club), the possessor of a rich baritone voice, met with high favour, his singing being warmly applauded. For "The Monks" he received an encore, .giving in response " The Village Blacksmith." Mr. J. W. Cotterill (Manchester) was in excellent voice and accorded sympathetic treatment to his contributions. Together , with Mr. Brown he gave the duet " The two beggars " for which they were heartily re-called. Miss Ona Ashton, a soprano of considerable meritlighly pleased the audience with her singing whilst the duet in which she was uncompelled by Mr. Brown, • and "The Gipsies' Laughing Trio" with Messrs. Brown and Cotterill were very effective. Mr. Wilson Holi, of Manchester, was the humorist and caused much ' laughter with his selections. Mr. Ernest Spafford was an efficient accompanist.</p>
        <p>you." There was fear in his eyes. He had seen the way this man had looked at Olive; he knew, of Course, that- under tho circumstances he would be very susceptible to woman's beauty. A great, a horrible dread entered his heart. "There is nothing to understand," Vernon I Salisbury answered—"nothing. I "—he ,looked I round him, and dropped his voiec—" feel sure this was the place. Don't go nearer, see how long . the grass grows. Your secret is quite safe—at all events for a time—and when he is found, if ever ho is, no one will connect you with the crime, unless I choose to speak—which 1. shall : not do if you behave properly to me. Let's go I back to the house now. John Brandon scanned the horizon quickly, then shuddered. In spite of the warning that had been given to him, he moved nem er to the ditch, looking down into it, trying, • and yet dreading, to pierce the thick curtain of grass and undergrowth that held its secret, glad that he failed. "You're sure," he asked, "that it was here? The cutting is nearly a mile long. How should you rtnueirbez the place ? The grass would have , bee 4 pressed down. I don't believe it was here." I He movpd even nearer; he stooped down. Vernon Salisbury caught his arm, pulled him backward. I "Come away ! he said, angrily. "Do you want to put your neck in the noose ? Do you suppose if anyone saw you istoopine• down like that they would not know yoltwere looking for somethin- ? Haven't you the sense to know curiosity Avoill induce them to look also ? Your safety and mine are honed up, so , have a care. I tell you the man is there, safely hidden. Do you think I should forget the spot where they nearly caught me again ? Come back to the hope; throw the thing otNyour mind. A pretty bridegroom you'll make! Pull yourself together, can't you? You haven't the nerve of a mouse. You've had the pluck to commit a crime for the sake of a woman, can't you summon up enough strength to keep the secret to yourself John Brandon 'turned without another word. After all, perhaps, this man was right: perhaps it would be better to go home. The body was evidently- safely hidden ; what was the good of looking for it ? To-day was the great day of his life, surely he could put anything else H out of hie mind, even the murder of a brother ! He wondered if he did look afraid--it was more than likely. Ile had been afraid, he would be so no longer ;.lie had allowed himself to think of the Itragedy or last night, when all his thoughts should have been given to the joy that was coming trt,,hini to-day. His love for Celia Verde was the master-passiou of his life; that being so, how was it that for a minute even he had allowed fear to dominate him ? In a moment a change seemed to come over him. The anxious, careworn look left hi % face; the pallor; he stepped more buoyantly; flung up b is head. I • "Nature has bees kind." be aaid—"lnts fileXo</p>
        <p>POLICE COURT. At this Court last Thursday. before Colonel J. Wood and Messrs. J. Robinson and J. C. Parr, Thomas Bullen, of Edrlestown was fined ss. for rising obscene language as he was leaving the Swan Hotel, on November 20th.— Elijah Cunliffe. of Oolborne, was fined IN. and costs for cutting away a window strap from a carriage on the Great Central hallway. between Lowton and Golborne. on -November 3rd. doing damage ;estimated at 2s. Defendant said he would not have done it had he been in his right senses.</p>
