        <head>Parochial Committee and the Lighting Question.</head>
        <p>Parochial Committee and the Lighting Question.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Stockton Heath Parochial Com- mittee was held on Tuesday evening in the Hall Cafe. Mr. George Pilling was in the chair, and there were also present :—Messrs. J. H. Clarl:e, J. Whitley, J. Fairhurst, T, R. Westbrook, J. W. Bonchier, W. Mclntyre, S. F. Welsby, F. Pearson. J. Hallows, H. J. Westbrook, G. F. Ashton (clerk), and J. Swinton (surveyor). The Clerk stated that in the matter of lighting the streets, he was asked to see the Town Clerk and ascer- tain whether the Corportain would agree to the Parochial Committee attaching meters themselves to some of the gas lamps to ascertain the actual cOnsump- tion per lamp. He was ineormed that the Town Clerk did not think there would be any, objection as long as the Corporation were not pot to any expense. Mr. Bouchier said he thought it would pay them to do their own lighting, but they could not decide until they knew what the consumption of gas was. He sug- gested that the Gas Committee put one or two meters one or two of the lamps with a view to deciding upon tbeir proposition that they should do their own lighting. (Hear, hear). On the 31st November the Lighting Committee went roupd the village, and out of 30 incandescent lights there were 16 not lighted. However, there had been a decided improvement during the past fortnight, and if they managed their own lighting there would be no further trouble. Mr. H. J. WESTBROOK said the reply of the Corpora- tion to their communications would lead one to believe that they had asked something unreasonable, but it was not so when there were 16 incandescent lights out, out of 30. Mr. Bouchier's suggestion was approved. NEW STREETS. It was reported that a draft order had been received from the Local Government, Board authorizing the mak- ing of new streets in Stockton Heath, and also about a dozen Passages; THE OPENING OF THE SWING BRIDGE. A meeting of the Parish- Council was held at the con- clusion of the Parochial Committee, Mr. Bouchier lore- siding. The Clerk (Mr. E. Bradshaw) read a letter from the Shia Canal Company' referring to the Council's letter ‘lf the Nth ult., respecting the inconvenience caused to the public by the opening of the Twenty Step Bridge t,," the passing of small. craft. The Company wrote mat the question was one about which they had had a correvondence with a number or owners of barges, and small craft which used the canal, the majority of which insisted upon the bridges being swung for their vessels on the plea that they could not lower their masts. In some eases they had found that to be the case. In others the cargo was sn loaded as to prevent, the masts being lowered, whilst in other cases the owners refused to lower the meets at night when that could he done as they said that prevented their showing their headlight, which was contrary to the Board of Trade regulations and caused risk of accidents should their vessels meet other craft after they had passed the bridge, and before they could raise their masts to show the light. The company were always desirous that the bridge should be worked so as to be opened for as short a time as possible, and on looking over the returns for the swinging of the bridge for the present year he found that although they had had some 5,868 swing, for the passing-of vessels only on 13 occasions had the bridge been opened against the pnblic for a period of ten minutes or over. Everything would be done by the company in order to give as little incon- venience as possible to the passenger and vehicular traffic passing over these swing bridges. The CLERK was instructed to write to the Ship Canal Company thanking them for their courteous letter. TREE PLANTING. A letter was read from Mr. G. F. Ashton, clerk to the 'Rural District Council of Runcorn, announcing that the Council approved the suggestion that instead of the whole of the Parish Council of Stockton Heath acting with the sub-committee appointed by the District Council to carry out the recommendations contained in the report of the Highways Exeentive Committee, the Parish Council appoint three of their number to join the said sob-eornmiteee in carrying out such recom- mendations. Each member of the Joint Committee would have the same power as any other member. Mr. Bouchier explained that all trees in Whiteaeld- road were to be placed 25 yards apart. In other streets they were to he 20 yards apart. On the North side of Whitefleld-read every alternate tree was to be removed. A letter was read from the Ship Canal Company con- senting to the Tree Planting Committee planting trees and shrubs along the banks of the canal, from the Cantilever'Bridge to the Stag Bridge on the understand- ing that certain conditions were agreed to, and that the committee obtained some responsible authority or person to guarantee that the proposals would be carried out. After a short discussion it was decided that the Parish Council should give the required guarantee for five years.</p>
        <p>WRSLEYA.N CIRTRCIL—ANNUAL GUILD SOCTAL.—On Monday night the annual social in connection with the Guild was held in the schoolroom. The first part of the programme was a miscellaneous concert,Mr.Harold London giving a few choice selections on his grams- phone. Miss Nellie Bennett and Miss Mason also sang with sweet expression two songs and Miss Mellow giving two recitations with marked effect. Refresh- ments were handed round to the members present and afterwards a picture post card competition took place. heir</p>
        <p>Mr. A. Ward heing tuCVarded a prize for naming `vd cut of the 28 cards presented for competition. The subse- quent proceedings consistod of an impromptu five minntes' speech, about 12 competitors entering. This I proved to be a very interesting competition,the manner '' in which the competitors handled the subject given to them, viz.. " Matches " provoking great amusement. ', The evening passed only too quickly and shortly after ten o'clock the social ended, the members having spent an enjoyable time together. /</p>
        <p>Dramatic Recitations. large audience assembled in the workmen's f-room at Messrs. Crosfield's on Monday evening, ?rofessor Richardson gave a series of dramatic itions. The programme was a highly interesting •including. scenes from The taming of the "—and as Much Appreciated.</p>
