      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>PRESENTATION TO MR. THOMAS ARMSTRONG.</head>
        <p>PRESENTATION TO MR. THOMAS ARMSTRONG.</p>
        <p>A Veteran Bandmaster.</p>
        <p>- The splendid services which Mr. Thomas Armstrong has rendered as bandmaster of the Blue Coat school for twenty-three years were fittingly recognized on Satur- day evening when he was presented by the "Old Blues" with a solid silver rose bowl and two silver vases. The I bowl wile inscribed :—" Presented to Mr. Thomas Armstrong, bandmaster at the Wartington Blue Coat ISchool since May, 1883, by his old pupils as a mark of their esteem, December 15, 1906.' The presentation took place in the chapel of the institution, the presence of a large gathering testifying to the high esteem in which Mr. Armstrong is held by those who have received the benefit of his careful tuition. The, pleasing duty of banding the gifts to Mr. Arrmstrong was allotted to Mr. Edward Rose, the respected bead-, master of the school, who in the course of a short speech apdlogized for the absence of several gentlemen, including Alderman Forshaw, the president of the Old Blues' Society, who said the speaker, had always taken a deep interest in the.band, and had given prizes for several years to the best players. (Applause.) Referring to Mr. Armstrong's wdrk,, Mr. Rose said no one but those who had bad experience of a boys' band could have any idea of the time, trouble, patience., and perseverance that were necessary in order to bring al band up to anything like a state af efficiency, owing to the continual changes which took place, as the boys, when they came of a certain age, were bound to leave, so that others might take their' places. Still he did not remember a single year when the band had not been up to the mark. (Applause.) (Applause).—Mr. Armstrong, in reply said be had con- ducted many bands, but he had derived more pleasure from teaching the band of the Blue Coat School than Ifrom any other band. (Hear, hear.)—During the even- ing an enjoyable musical programme was submitted including an organ solo by Mr. T. R. Lane (organist of Darwen Parish Church and formerly of Walton), songs by Miss Lily Snelling and Mr. W. Broughton, trombone solos by Mr. E. Smith, a cornet solo by Mr. W. Butter and an instrumental selection by Messrs. W. Rutter, G. B. Smallwood, J. Lowe and T. Rowley (Mr. E. Smith condticting). All taking part in the programme were "Old Blues." Before separating, Mr. T. B. Lane spoke of the value of the work of Mr. Armstrong as a band- master. and gave an interesting personal experience on first joining the Blue Coat School band. He mentioned that his father was the first bandmaster of the Warring- ton Volunteer Band. ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>DANGER OF NEGLECTED COLDS. The Worst Cold Yields to the Pure</head>
        <p>DANGER OF NEGLECTED COLDS. The Worst Cold Yields to the Pure</p>
        <p>Pine-Air in Peps.</p>
        <p>Our humid and changeable climate seems to favour the development of those numerous maladies known under the common name of "colds." Bronchial colds, pulmonary colds, malarial colds, feverish colds, colds in the bead, and "shivering colds," are all closely related, and all so familiar that there is a danger of making too little of them and by neglecting proper precautions, inviting serious constitutional mischief, or even fatal consequences. The introduction of Peps, the new pine-air treatment for coughs and colds and all pulmonary troubles, has brought a truly scientific home remedy within the reach of all. The results of neglecting an ordinary cold are often extremely serious. Neglect a cold in the head and the result may be chronic catarrh, deafness, constant running at the nose, a harsh cough, and chronic sore throat. Neglet of a cold on the chest brings about winter cough, bronchitis, and a weak chest, even if lung inflammation, pleurisy or pneumonia does not supervene Even though a cold may seem to have been shaken off, it often leaves a weakness behind, and a tendency to take cold again more readily than other people. This is especially the case when a cold has been treated with old - fashioned syrupy cough-mixtures (often heavily drugged). The Peps treatment for colds introduces the healing breath of the rich pine-woods into the throat, chest, and lungs. The delicate lining membranes are bathed in the soothing and healing odours released from Peps as 'they slowly dissolve on the tongue. When there are colds in the house take Peps to defend you against infection. If you have caught cold take Peps promptly and freely, and you will not only shake it off, but will escape the serious consequences. If you are suffering from the results of a neglected cold, from winter cough, bronchitis, asthma,chronic catarrh (or "hay fever 1, lung-weakness, laryngitis, relaxed throat, or that nasty chest cough which keeps you awake at nights, Peps will bring you certain and speedy relief. Of all chemists, at is. lA. or 2s. 9d. per box. or post free at the same prices direct from the Peps Pastille Co., Carlton Hill, Leeds.</p>
