        <p>"Very worried, and he has been °Rea in London of late." "I hope there is nothing wrong?" said Jane, anxiously. Ellen smiled faintly', knowing that the question referred to money.. "I do not think so. Mr. Horneastle is a clever speculator; and is not likely to lose." Walter and Jane exchanged ,glances, for, as the squire was teetotal, both gentlemen had followed the ladies when they retired. " I heard that something was wrong," stammered Walter. ," Then we must ask Mr. Horncastle if there is truth in what you heard," observed George Caine. Waltero.a coward at heart, looked alarthed. "I would not dare .to speak to the squire about his private affairs. He would resent my advising him in any way." Considering that Mr. Raleigh was a fool, it was very probable that the squire would resent his advice, and in a drastic manner. However, there were so many rumours of monetary difficulties, as detailed by Walter. that the daughters decided to speak to their father. Then the quartette wrangled as to who should bell the cat. Ellen listened for a time, sick at heart over their sordid quarrels, and then slipped out of the room quietly. In ten minutes, warmly clothed, she was taking her way over the snow under a frosty sky to meet her own true love; who had come over the seas for love of her. In the shadbw of the tower stood a slim .figure, silent and still. With the faithful instinct of a loving woman, Ellen knew him on the instant, and ran forward. Then, with his arms round her, did he kiss the cheeks that had, lost their roses for his hake. "My own darling ! " "You still love mn, then? " she murmured, scarcely believing that so great a joy could enter her sad life. " Does not this prove it, and this, and this, and this." Ellen withdrew herself from his kisses. " I am growing old. I have lost my looks," she</p>
        <p>faltei:ed. "You are ray own keart's delight," said Jack, grasping her hands and drawing her into the moonlight. It etherealised her, and gave her back a lost youth. He gazed into her flushed face, looked deep into her eyes, that shone like the stars above, And marked—as of yore—the little curls he loved, that clung round her neck. aMy heart—my own—you are a thousand times more beautiful ! " and he kissed her again, with a true lover's kiss. So ,passed a few 'minutes, until the church clock struck the quarter to ten. She 'dis- engaged herself hurriedly. "I must go in; it is late. 0h!- Jack "—she held him by the coat with both hands—"why cannot you come to the Manor to see your father—after all these years, it is but right." "And risky—unless my father has soft- ened." "Alas ! Alas !" "I understand. He still looks upon me as a prodigal. Well, so I am. See—he stepped still further into the searching moon- light and spread oat his arms—" I have but this suit, and enough money to take me back to exile." The clothes were old and worn, the face haggard. He looked like a man under a cloud. "You have not succeeded?" she faltered. " 1 am a prodigal," said he; "and will you —ah ! I knew it. She had flung her arms round his neck and was fondling him tenderly. "You are mine —rich or poor, you are mule! But if you could see your father, he might forgive." "No ! I to England to see you. I go</p>
        <p>,40 came _ away again soon." " Alone? " It was a cry from her heart. "That is for you to decide," and Ile eyed her steadily. She wrung her hands, sobbing. "What can I do—what should I do? You are poor, I have no a---.Jney; your father will never con- sent." "His consent is not required." "No, no! I understand. Still, there is a chance that he might forgive; but no, he is so hard—so cruel." Jack took a letter from his pocket. "As you say, there is a chance—this chance," said he, and pressed it into her hand. "What is it?" "Read, and you will understand." "I cannot read here. Let me take it home, and—" "And give it to my father." "To your father?" She looked in the thin cold light at the letter. It was 'in a woman's handwriting, and the paper was old, yellow, worn at the edges. "It is my mother's writinf" said Jack, in . t</p>
        <p>asked Ellen,</p>
        <p>never tried to win their.' hearts. Seeing that the master of the house was absent, everyone, children and elders, enjoyed themselves; but the, sadness came again with dinner', when the squire, glooming like an unbidden guest, sat at the head of the table. Afterwards, when the children danced into the hall to see the glittering tree, with its load of gifts, he stopped his daughters and their husbands with a, word. "I wish you to drink," said he, raising a glass of water, "to to-morroiv—the last Christmas Day you will spend here." "Father ! " cried Flora and, Jane, in a breath, and turning white. "The last Christmas Day," repeated Horn- castle, with a grim smile, "for I am— ruined." He flung the word in their faces, and it was echoed. " Ruined ! " "Yes, I speculated, and I have lost. Every- thing is gone from me save a pittance on which to. live. And now "—he glared at them with ironic merriment—" let us be joyful on this happy Christmas Eve." Then even he unbent, when called upon that strange Christmas Eve to discri- bute the fruit of,. the tree. , Cynic and misanthrope as he was, he could not repel the innocent desire of the children that he should be their Santa Claus. Consequently, everyone beheld the astonishing spectacle of a grim old man bestowing toys on small boys and girls with, for him, quite a genial smile. But he declined to give the elders their presents, and delegated his post of distribu- tor to Ellen. Thus it came about that he NN as greatly surprised to receive a present him- _ Such a thing had never happened to</p>
        <p>him before. "And what is this?" Ile asked, looking askance at the sealed envelope. "You must open and see—open and see," cried the children, dancing. Horncastle obeyed with a tolerant air, pre- pared for some foolish surprise. The atten- tidn of the children was attracted by many new toys, and the elders were busy receiving their presents. Thus all eyes were with- drawn from Horncastle, and the groan to which he gave vent on beholding the worn letter made everyone look at him. He recognised his late wife's handwriting, and also saw that it was addreSsed to Frank Trent, her, first husband, in some far distant s:ear. "Papa!" cried Jane, frightened by his looks. Horncastle stared vaguely at Ellen. " Where did you get this? ' "Go into the library and read it," she said, quietly. "I sholl come and tell you shortly." Unaceustomed to be ordered about, the ^ hesitated But he was too much</p>
