        <p>"Where—did---</p>
        <p>agony of soul, divided between doing- right • I "Can you wonder, When she made so itnd living 'wrong, was apparent in every line, great a sacrifice?" There was a soul to be saved, and the woman "lit was a mistaken sacrifice; none was re- I sacrificed herself to save it. The choice was " apparently accepted by the first husband, as quired. Ellen judged from what Jack had told her. "Nevertheless, believing ,Captain Trent's I Trent's wife stopped with Horncastle, without assertion that she was his wife, it was a i i letting him know that her first husband lived, sacrifice. She chase to remain with you and so saved him from a drunkard's grave. because—" And by an irony of circumstances this very Horncastle raised his head, struck by the Trent had -died a year ago in the arms of his sudden pause she made. A flush of colour wife's son, but not his crept into his ashen cheeks, and his mouth own. Ellen guessed the 'effect that this letter quivered. He tried vainly to meet the would have on Squire Horncastle. Seeing woman's eye, but could not. With his chin that he was here set forth as a reclaime sunken on his breast, with lowered oyes, and drunkard, that ' .in these pathetic lines he shaking figure, he looked and felt like a could read the enormous sacrifice the woman ' culprit bekre a clear-eyed judge. , had made, surely he would no longer with- "It is true, then," said Ellen, pitilessly; hold his pardon from a soli who, at the worst, "it sis true that you were a drunkard—a pro- had been but wild. He was not without sin fligate—a—" himself, therefore coulet he cast no stOnes. ' "I waa all that was bad," cried the man, The woman—poor Ellen, anxious and eitger vehemently. "She saved me, But for her to right the wrong, pondered for many an I would have gone down into the pit, hour over the letter. Then she made- up her into the depths of shame and degrada- mind to deliver it to the squire in a season- tion. She knew what I was before able way, and after her own fashion. , In J' she called me husband; but she took pursuance of. her plan she wrote to my life on her shoulders and shamed me Jack, telling him to be on the terrace, at with tenderness into decency and temper- the library window, the next night ,at eleven. mice. Oh, Laura—Laura—my soul—my life Possibly there would be a dramatic 'scene, but my dead darling—how I have missed you and hardly a tragic one, seeing that as things cried for you! Will we ever meet again— stood it was bound to end happily—and on ever—ever—ever? " Christmas Eve too, fit time for joyful end- "No," said Ellen, loudly, and the old man ings. ' recoiled as though she had struck him a At the Manor the old custom' of stripping blow. "No ! For you have forgotten the the Christmas-tree on Christmas • Eve was mercy of God." observed, the cereinony taking place after "The mercy of God ! " cried the man, dinner. For time most part of the next day fiercely. "What mercy did He shew to me Ellen was busy 'getting ready for the when He took away my prop—or to her, evening' S entertainment, assisted by Jane when he allowed her to suffer through this and Flora. These ladies grumbled not useless sacrifice ! " and he struck the letter. a little at the disagreeable way in "Did not God shew you mercy when He which their father was behaving. Horn- gave you a pure and good woman to wife, castle remained in his library hour after hour, that you might be saved from the depths of admitting no one. But he promised to be degradation? You used the phrase yourself— present at the stripping of the tree. The the depths of degradation. God sent you children did not mind this seclusion, as they His angel, and lifted you up. What mercy had little love for ,a grandfather who had did He shew to you? Why, that mercy. And</p>
        <p>what mercy have you sheWn, Mr. Horitcastle? None !" , The word rang out like a trumpet note. "What do you mean?" he asked, aghast. "Your son—her son—Jack—my lover." "You ask me to forgive him?" Ellen pointed to the letter. "Your dead wife—his mother—asks you to forgive, and God commands it. You call Jack a profli- gate and a drunkard. Even were he so— which he never was—who are you to decry in him those shameful sins of which you. have yourself been guilty. The stones that might have been cast at you, and were not, you have cast at him. God shewed Mercy to you, and you—you—hard-hearted old man, unjust, and cruel—how have you treated the child of the woman who made so mighty a sacrifice for you?" "Ellen !" He stood with a bloodless face, and widely open eyes, staring at the trans- formed creature whom he beheld before him. And well he might. No longer did he see the meek, uncomplaining woman, who had borne insults, and loneliness, and sorrow these many bitter years; but, a queen, an accuser, who denounced him in words which cut like a whip. • "Yes," Ellen cried, her voice strong, her face flushed, and her eyes brilliant, "you ruined my life—you ruined Jack's—you con- demned us to sorrow and solitude for years and years—oh! many hard and bitter years. You did—yes, you—the sinner—the man who could accept such a sacrifice ! " "No, no!" he cried, is agony, and clasped his hands. " I swear I never knew that Trent had written. I would have spoken, but I never knew that she suffered."</p>
        <p>"You know now. What do you say?" "I have been wrong—wrong." "You are still wrong, and will be till you forgive your son." "Yes—yes. I shall do so. God forgive me. I shall write—l—" "There is no need." Ellen stepped up to the near French window and flung it open. "Jack gave me the letter. Trent died in Jack's arms, and Jack is here." In the moonlight stead the wanderer. "Father," he said—" father! Horricastle gazed on him in alarm and astonishment, and retreated as though he, be- held a spectre. " John ! John! No, no!" "Yes," said Ellen, standing between the old man and the young. "He has come home to marry me." There was a note of triumph in her voice. "And to ask my father's forgiveness," supplemented Jack. "No !" Horncastle tittered a loud cry and flung himself on his knees in abject supplica- tion. "It is I—l who have to be forgiven. Oh, my son—my son John, pardon me. I</p>
        <p>„ had mercy she 'n to me, and to you—her flesh and blood—l -shewed none.” " Father 1 " Jack, much distressed, lifted the old man to his feet. " Let us forgive one another on this Christmas Eve." "There is nothing to forgive in you, John. You never were as wicked and evil as I was —as I am, God help Inc. I drank hard— and your mother saved me." "God bless her! " "And me, John. Say, God bless me. I can make no reparation," he wailed, "I am poor. I have lost all—all, even the Manor." Jack look startled, then led his father to a chair and soothed him gently. "It does not matter," said he, quietly; " come with me to the South Seas. lam rich enough to keep you in eomfort." "Jack," cried Ellen, astonished, said you were poor." "So -I am—seeing I have not you to wife." He laughed at the sight of her puzzled face. "But I made my fortune out of copra in the South Seas. I am very well off indeed. These clothes and my talk were but to test you. You have stood the test well." " HoW. cruel you are," said Ellen, almost tearfully; " as though I wanted any testing. I am true—l have always been true." "And I false," moaned the old man. "Take her, John—take - a good wife in ex- change for a bad father." "1 hope to have both," said Jack, with his arm round Ellen's waist. , Horncastle hesitated. " There is one thin you must know, John. Your dear mother— ' " I do know," interrupted the young man, hastily; "you spoke so loudly that I could not help hearing. I know that my mother was really and truly your wife, although she made that mistaken sacrifice. Ah ! what a villain that Trent was to the last. He never told me the truth—no, not even when he was dying. The villain!' "No greater than I am—not so great; no —no, not so great." "You have made amends," cried . Ellen, impetuously. "I can never make amends. I have been cruel, and wicked, and I denied 'to my son- that mercy which God shewed to me." "I forgive you with all my heart," said Jack, distressed by this anguish. "Do not trouble any more, father. Forget—forget." "But " God—God, against whom I have offended so grievously. Will He forgive? " and Horneastle wrung his hands. Hush! " Ellen threw up her hand. The answer was coming, but not from any human being. Far away in the interior of the house boomed a deep-toned clock. It was midnight, and Ellen, running to the win- dow, reopened it. A 'breath of frosty air swept in, and then Jacl, led his father gently on to the terrace, where they stood on the frozen snow, and under the hard glittering stars. The sudden clash and clamour of bells made music through- the still, windless night. God answers you, father," murmured Jack, softly. "Great is my sin," said Horneastle'. "And great is your repentance." "But greater than all is the forgiveness ct God," said Ellen, solemnly. " Christ is bora for us all three, this holy night." And the squire stood between his son ant daughter—a changed man holding a hand di each. Louder and more jubilantly rang out th,3 bells with their message a peace ant pardon. "The prodigal's Christmas," said Jack, laughing. Like a strain of grave music came the voice of Ellen, reneatina the noble words of St. "But when he was yet a great way off, his father saw him, and had compassion, and fell on his neck and kissed him." "He has—he has," cried the old man, with joy and tearful thankfulness. "For it is I who am the prodigal. But I have come home to my Father, and He has forgiven nate..'t rrgs</p>
        <p>"you</p>
