      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>PRODIGAL'S . CHRISTMAS.</head>
        <p>THE PRODIGAL'S . CHRISTMAS.</p>
        <p>By FERGUS 1-lUME,</p>
        <p>Atuthor of "The Mystery /of a Hansom Cab," "Island of Fantasy," "Indian Bangle," "The Golden Wang-he," "Madame Midas," &amp;c.</p>
        <p>"A letter from the South Seas," said the hard-faced, ' grey-headed man, glooming dangerously.</p>
        <p>. _ "From Jack?" The woman, who was pouring out the coffee, looked up quickly, with a startled expression on her patint Lace. _</p>
        <p>"From my ,son , John—the scamp, the prodigal, the rascal ! " "And my lover." "You dare to say that—to my face! " "As you hear." She passed a cup. coffee, Mr. Horricastle."</p>
        <p>"Your</p>
        <p>The old man took it mechanically, and breathed hard. "You shall never marry him, Ellen." "I think so, if he comes and fetches me." "Let him dare to enter the house he has disgraced, and I shall shew him the door. I forbid yoa to----" "I decline to accept your bidding," said Ellen, in 'a low tone. I am your war, truly, or rather, was. I am now Over age.' "Very much over age," sneered the other. "That taunt falls harmless, Mr. Horn. castle. I own up to my thirty years without hesitation. As to my face "—she glanced into the shining surface of the silver coffee-pot— "it is faded to you; but to Jack—to my Jack " she smiled fondly. "A scamp, a ra,scal, I tell you." "And my lover," said she again. He glanced at her vengefully, then opened the letter with trembling hands. Even as he did so, Ellen Tait felt in her pocket' with a bewildered look; what she touched reassured her, and she smiled in a faint, relieved manner.</p>
        <p>Ellen Tait was beAtlt,iful ; but less with the vitality of youth than the calm peace of middle age, that has suffered much and meditated constantly. Her hair and eyes were dark, her mouth drooping and sorrow- ful, and there were faint wrinkles on her forehead, which should, by rights, have come many years later. The tragedy of a hungry heart, of an unsatisfied life, was appar- ent in her patient looks and spiritless actions. Poor Ellen Tait! For fifteen years she had loved Jack Horncastle, and for ten had she been engaged to marry him, against the will and wish of his father. It seemed incredible, even to one of her hopes, that he should ever clasp her in his arms and call her wife.</p>
        <p>The squire himself was old and stern, a hard landlord and father, and a domestic tyrant of the worst. His wife had died many years since, and with her passed any softening influence which would have made him more human. He had loved her with all the passion of which his nature was capable, and therefore had none to spare for the son she had left behind. The boy was high-spirited, and—like all young animals —a trifle wild. But his escapades had never been sufficient to justify exile, and alienation from all that he held near and dear. Horn. castle was what the Scotch call "dour," and could not forgive the lad's overflowing spirits. When Jack annou.nced that .he intended to marry Ellen \who loved him fondly, as he did her, the squire's wrath drove him from home, from England, and for 'ten years had exiled him in far countries. Jack wrote to Ellen, who still remained faithful, but never to his father. Hence the look of surprise on the woman's face when Horncastle announced the letter.</p>
        <p>"Wants us to be friends," growled the squire, crushing up the letter and tossing it into the fire. "He's meek and humble, is he? Ahr-r-r ! he'll be. more humble before he looks on my face." "To-morrow night is Christmas Eve," Ellen reminded him, and winced as the olive. branch withered in the flames. "Don't I know that," snarled Horneastle. "Isn't the house filled with my grandchildren —Jane and Flora's brats? Aren't they conil\ in g down to-morrow with their fools of hus- bands to keep Christmas? Bah! I hate all such things. Well "—he chuckled unpleas- antly—" I have a surprise for them." " What is it? ' she asked, absently. "A surprise for you also, Ellen. To-morrow, after the mummery of the Christmas-tree, or perhaps after dinner, you shall hear.:' "I hope the surprise is a pleasant one? " Horneastle snarled again and scowled. "Oh," he mocked, " doubtless you hope I shall bring Jack into the room, and let you kiss him under the mistletoe. You needn't think it, even if he wee here instead of far away in the South Seas." "Are you so sure he is there ? " she in- quired, and felt spin in her pocket—perhaps for a handkerchief. '</p>
        <p>The old man pushed away his plate—he had eaten nothing—and rose to pace the, room. " Sure ! *Why*, the letter • there "—he 'nodded towards the fire—" has been written only three months back. Had he 'been coming home he would not have sent that. And he can stay away," he cried, with sudden. fury— , " the idle, drunken vagabond ! " "He is not," said Ellen, called upon to defend her absent lover. "He is not. He works day and night to make a fortune. Drunken—he was a trifle wild, but nothing to speak of. A vagabond—and who has made him so but his own father, who should have cherished him for his mother's sake if not or his own? " I I The squire glared at her furiously, and would have burst out into a long tirade, but that the word mother struck home. His voice took on a quieter tone, and his eyes grew, dim. "His mother," Saki he, with a sigh of regret! "Ali ! she was a good woman when I married her. - When her husband, Captain Trent, was lost in the Pacific she became my wife. How could. she—of all women—come to • have a profligate drunkard for a son." "He is not," cried Ellen again; "you ex- aggerate. Jack was wild, but he is now thirty-five, and sober—yes, and industrious, and sorry, that he should be estranged from' you. That letter—why 'did you burn it? " " Because I'll have nothing to do with the writer.. And do not refer to his mother again. She was a—a—a saint." Something clinked in his throat. " But for her, I have Here he waved his hand hastily and , went out of the room, apparently draid to trust himself to further speech. Ellen glanced at the closed door, and listened to the distant laughter of children. The house was filled with them ten at least, • four of Jane's and six of Flora's. With their • nurses they had arrived on the • previous day to spend Christmas. Their mothers----the squire's daughters—would come that day with their husbands. .It was strange that a hard man like the squire should tolerate Christmas revelry, even to .the extent of a tree, and should welcome his daughters and their families when he was so bitter , against Jack. She could not 'understand this contra- diction, any more than she could understand why Jack had written.'. The letter that she produced .from her pecket, to read hurriedly, explained her perplexity, for it was from jack himself, written in London on the previous day, and stating, that he would come that night secretly to Horncastle village to see - her. This being so, Ellen could not under- stand why a letter to his father bearing the post-mark of the South Seas should arrive almost at the same time. In vain did she</p>
        <p>wrinkle her brows over this problem. All that clay the children trooped after her, for she was their great favourite. Willing as he was to open his house to the little people, Horncaotle did not Care much for them or or their parents. He remained in his library, took lonely walks in the park, and even when his daUghters arrived did not greet them until they entered the dining. room. • "Flora! Jane ! " He peeked both on the cheeks. "•WaAter ! George ! " He shook hands coldly with the men. " I am glad to 3ee you." But his grumtiness all through iinner ballad his words. rapa seems to be breaking up,— said Flora to Ellen, in the drawing-room. "I o-evar, paw. him so cross. Is he .worried tt</p>
      </div>
