      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>DIICEMBBR 22 1906.</p>
        <p>A MERRY CHRISTMAS AND A HAPPY NEW YEAR TO ALL OUR READERS!</p>
        <p>The Education Bill is dead 66 THE PEOPLE —it was " damned " long SHALL before in the place where PREVAIL." it has finally been foully murdered, i.e. the House of Lords. Strange to say, however, its best friends are not sorrowing exceedingly. Many of them had doubts whether,in its altered form,the Bill was worth savirg. We have already given expression to our views on this subject. In order to mak3 a strong position for itself the Government was bound to go to the utmost limits of possible Con- cession. It has done so, and without any result except one which corresponds to the clearing of the decks for action in the case of a fleet. But the decks are cleared. The Government has made no false move. It Ir as given every possible oppor- tunity to the House of Lords. Lord LANsnowNes objection to the fashion in which the Bill was returned to the Upper House may be simply swept away. At most it amounts to the assertion of an offended amour propre. The situation is too serious to permit of discussion as to whether the representative body should approach the Peers on "bended kness and with whispering humbleness, or should, as actually happened, state point blank that it was impossible to accept their proposals. The real question at issue is whether the party beaten at the polls is to continue to rule the country in everything but the granting of 4upplies. This is capable of one answer only—the House of Lords, inspired by M. BALFOUR, cannot be per- mitted to dam back the tide of Liberal legislation. The Peers seem to have no appreciation of the actual feeling of the country. •To be in London is to be fsr.removed from any place in which it is</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <p>possible to feel the beating of the national pulse. There seems to be an idea among Mr. BALFOUR'S friends and puppets that the wave of the General Election has spent itself ; that it has a great and continuing flood of national feeling behind it seems to be something quite beyond their appre- hension. They are, we imagine, about to be shaken into anew consciousness of the power and persistence of opinion among the people. Lord CREWE'S speech in the House of Lords seemed to indicate that the Bill would be abandoned in favour of a drastic:administration of the Education Acts, Probably that is. the best course to follow for the time being. Administri- tion that is merely just and consistent, that is applied to one school on the same terms as to another, will make the ARCHBISHOP OF CANTER- BURY very sick and sorry. The course adopted by the House of Lords has been anything but contributory to the advantage of the voluntary schools. The offers that were rejected on Wed- nesday were the best offers that will ever be made. They went immensely beyond any compromise that. would satisfy any section of the supporters of the Government. They converted what was meant for a final into what :could only have been a temporary settlement. As they were not accepted the Government must now fall back on what Sir HENRY CAMPBELL-BANNERMAN has described as "another way." What that other way will be remains to be seen, but there can be little doubt that from to-day commences that agitation which Mr. GLADSTONE invited the country to undertake in the concluding words of his last speech in Parliament more than twelve years ago. The struggle between Peers and People has suddenly become imminent, and in the words of the PRIME MINISTER, " thelpeople shall prevail." Sir HENRY CAMPBELL-BANNERMAN rose to the occasion on Thursday in a fashion which compels admiration. He led as a leader should—with the banner held aloft and a bold front to the foe. "At the bidding of a party which had been condemned at the General Election as no other party had ever been condemned before, the House of Lords had killed the Govern- ment's Bill. Is the General Election and its results to go for nothing ?" he asked, and the answer was a storm of cheering which left no doubt as to the temper of the House. "NA way mist be found and will be found to make the will of the people prevail," said the Prima Minster. His words will be echoed in every corner of the land. The people have been flouted time and again by the hereditary legislators who are always willing to do the bidding of the Bishops and the Brewers as representing the dominant forces in English Conservatism. They are openly contemptuous of the mandate which was given to i the House of Commons, and have embarked upon a destructive policy which cannot and vill not be tolerated. The Lords have mutilated the Land Tenure Biil and the Irish Town Tenants Bill. They have attempted, though unsuccessfully, to mutilate the Trade Disputes Bill, and have finally only let it pass amid muttered curves. They have thrown out the Plural Voting Bill and the Education Bill. The whole Government programme of a year lies in ruins before their sudden activity. With ignorance. of the questions at issue, animated as it seems, by a kind of blind hatred of the action of the Commons, a small body—in most cases leas than a hundred and fifty Peers—have set them- selves to destroy the work of the new party. If that party were to accept such a verdict as final they might roll up the map of progress for a generation. Far more speedily than many would have wished and most would have prophesied, the constitutional issue 'suddenly occupies all the arena. And that tissue, thus defiantly raised, must now be fought to the end.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Savings SAVINGS BANK Bank is pre-eminently a PROSPERITY, working -class institution' and its fortunes therefore may be taken as some criterion of the material , prosperity of the community. From the state. ments submitted at the annual meeting it is gratifying to know that the working of the bank during the past year has been attended with increased success, and there has been a steady advance in the number of accounts and trans- actions. As the large proportion of these are small amounts it is evident that the facilities offered by the institution are being appreciated by those for whom they are mainly intended. l'he special investment department has already justified its existence, over 22.000 having been received or transferred during the year. The wisdom of opening branch banks at Bank Quay and Earles- town has also been strikingly demonstrated, the deposits at both establishments being most satis- factory, and the managers are to be congratulated on the enterprise which has convenienced the public and brought increased business.</p>
        <p>Our "happy aphorism " BELLMONT SCHOOL. to use Canon Stevenson's description—that "no local men need apply" for headmasterships in the new Council schools has been justified by the facts and the sequel. The Education Committee on Monday ' evening by a two to one vote confirmed the ' Beamont School managers' appointment of Mr. Mason, of Eccles, as headmaster. Points for and ' against were very capably emphasised in the dis- cussion, and the one thing generally admitted was that local men were entitled to a preferential consideration in these matters. It seems quite clear to us that while the preference was agreed to it wa. never granted, either by the managers of the school or the majority of the full com- mittee. There were seventeen local applicants for the post, and fourteen were ruled out straightway because they had only taken a second class pass instead of a first. An authority on the question like Mr. Upson Bays the difference is infinitesimal, while even Canon Stevenson 'admits the room for a difference of opinion as to the wisdom of making such a rule. But the three men who were first-class fared little better. Instead of exercising their prerogative of giving a fair preference to local candidates the managers seem to have sought for a reason for excluding the lot. They found it in the fact that even the two best of the local men had not had any previous experience of the headship of an import- ant school. This was the last and the insuperable bar. In our view it is the weakest excuse of any for appointing an outsider. If the local assistant- masters are to be bowled out on such a pretext, where is their remedy? They cannot get the " experience " unless they go else- where apparently. Promotion at home is denied them. This want of trust in the capability of men whose work has been under notice for years and whose worth is unquestioned cannot be too strongly condemned. It is not even defensible by ordinary common-sense reasoning. It is almost invariably the case both in our business and municipal life to promote the assistant or deputy when the head of a depart- ment resigns or is removed. As an illustration, when the Town Clerkship of a Borough becomes vacant the deputy always has the first chance if he is a competent man. It is no bar to his chances that he has not been Town Clerk some- where else previously. The most natural and reasonable thing for the Education Committee to have done in the present circumstance was to have appointed the best of their numerous capable assistant masters. By their inconsiderate and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="3" unit="words">
