        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, December 22, 1906.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, December 22, 1906.</p>
        <p>even unfair action they have embittered th 3 whole of the local teaching profession, which is surely a poor way of farthering educational interests. Furthermore they have practically placed promotion by merit on an unapproachable pedestal and at the same time injured the pros. poets of local men who are prepared to enter into competition for a post in another town. We do not doubt that the managers have secured a good man in Mr. Mason, but we believe there are men in Warrington who would have conducted the Beamont School with all the success that could be delqired. To presume anything else is an insult to some of our best public servants, and we much regret that the authorities have not, in common fairness, accorded that local preference which the circumstances and the feeling in the town alike demandel.</p>
        <p>The day of free education SCHOOL FEES. all round has not dawned yet, end even the energetic push from the Finance Committee of the Education Authority has not hastened the dawning. This committee seems to have acted somewhat hastily in recommending the abolition of all school fees in the town simply because of the Board of Educa- tion's refusal to sanction fees in the new Beamont School. It might even be concluded with good reason that the Committee was somewhat piqued and had said to itself : Well if we can't charge fees in the new school we will free all the rest and the ratepayers must bear the cost—a sort of indirect slap at the Board of Education. The latter is rather thick-skinned and might not nave cared either for the slap or the obvious sufferings of the Warrington rate- payers. Fortunately—and we say this not because we do not favour free education—the Finance Committee's desperate plan was defeated and things will go on as before except that there will be one school more at which no fees can be charged. There are various weighty 'reasons why the schools should not be placed on the same levell all at once, and one of them is found hidden under ' those tine fresh phrases "social exclusion " and "subsidized snobbery." It is not unreasonable for the respectable and fairly well-to-do papa to desire to send his children to an elementary school where the percentage of rough, uncouth scholars is infinitesimal, especially as he is quite willing to pay for the privilege in the form of a fee.A supersensitive democrat might see in this the germs of snobbery, but the difficulty is real and entitled to earnest consideration. Granted an even number of " nice " boys and " naughty " boys in a school, the former could not be relied upon to retain all their excellencies and at the same time raise the level of the others. On the contrary,experienc9 teaches that the influence of the " naughty " boys would be likely to permeate the school more quickly even than the measles. ' Universal equality has not yet arrived, and while we agree that education should be free to all, there must be some means of making that distinction which parents have a right to claim.</p>
        <p>It is, of course, the tritest OUR YELLOW ALLY, of common-places to say that the Japanese are a wonderful people, but at almost every step they take in international affairs we find ourselves saying it again with increased emphasis. There are two incidents at present before the world which illustrate this point. In the first place it seems probable that Japan's controversy with the Russian Government as to the interpretation of the terms of the Treaty of Portsmouth will be submitted to international arbitration. The second and more striking incident is in con- nection with the controversy concerning the admission of Japanese children to the State schools of California. They accept President Roosevelt's. apologies for his inability to secure for them what he admits is their just due, owing to the unfortunate racial prejudice of the Californians and, it is reported, will meet the difficulty by over- coming that prejudice. The Japanese Govern- ment will encourage and assist its subjects to build their own schools in San Francisco and in other places where like difficulties arise, and they intend that these institutions shall be such models of efficiency as to excite the admiration of the Americans, and so overcome their ill-feeling. They are determined to do this with their own money, and not to accept any help from sympathetic Americans. Such a project can but excite our admiration. Having beaten Russia at the game of war, they mean to conquer Uncle Sam by the arts of peace. This ambition is all to their credit. Japan, despite her remarkable pro- gress, has still to a great extent to work out her destiny among the newer peoples of the world, less ready, curiously enough, to aceept her enhanced position than are the nations of Europe.</p>
        <p>*s* TO UNIONIST OWNERS OF REAL AND PER- SONAL ESTATE—WAKE UP! The priest-ridden Peers, and our weak, Halifax-obsessed, Macdonnell- tarred leaders, are maiming their power to stay rapine, and are throwing to the wolves your most solid interests 'to save and uphold an unpopular clericalism The Extremists, moreover, habitually vote Radical and Home Rule and are more than tainted with 'Socialism. PROTEST ENERGETICALLY! Withhold subscrip- tions and motor cars until remonstrance is heeded. INDIGNANT UNIONIST. —An agmy advertisement in the " Times." *s* Twenty-three years as bandmaster of the Blue Coat school is a record of which Mr. Thomas Armstrong has every reason to feel proud. The presentation on Saturday evening testified to the affection with which he is regarded by "Old Blues" who have passed through his hands. To teach a boys' band requires exemplary patience but Mr. Armstrong has done it all with such kindness and good humofir as to make the musical lessons one of the most interesting and pleasant parts of the school curriculum. ks was well pointed out, local bands are under an obligation to him for having provided them with many useful recruits. *s*</p>
        <p>If the Warrington school teachers are not actually up in arms, they are certainly full of resentment at the slight which has been put upon the this week by the Education Committee. The decision to import a headmaster for the first of the new schools and put all local applicants on one side suggests that they are all either incompetent or at any rate not worth promotion in the ordinary way. The managers who first made the momentous selection are Councillor&amp; Ball, Lord, Pemberton, Fulton-Smith, Wilkinson and Canon Stevenson. Of these six only the two" Non-Traders" fought and voted for a local appointment. They are used to being in a minority ! * * It was the misfortune of the Agricultural Society to have a wet day for their last annual show and the result was seen in a largely decreased " gate " and a correspondingly increased deficit. The President, however, has once more come to the rescue and his special subscription of £lBO enables the Society to show a balance in hand. As one speaker put it, the Society would no doubt have been bankrupt 'ere this had it not been for Sir Gilbert's interest and support. It is hoped that the extension of the radius decided upon at the annual meeting will tend to strengthen the Society's financial position, and that better luck will be experienced at the next show.</p>
        <p>* The following are two of the latest opinions ton the suffragette question : "As a means of temporary flirtation and evanescent love-making, destined to end with the end of the election, women's suffrage could hardly be surpassed. I Perhaps this, after all, is the real object of the impetuous movement."—Miss Marie Corelli, in the " Rapid Review." , " For twenty years it has been an axiom of the Radical wirepuller that the rejection of one man one vote by the tipper House meant the end of the peers. Why have all these calculations miscarried? The answer is Miss Christobel Pankhurst. Were these demonstrations attempted, the suffragettes would hang on the flank of the demonstrators and make them ridiculous."—" Saturday Review." • Complaints having been 'made to the Ship Canal Company about the frequent opening of the Stockton Heath swing bridge for small craft, the Company has replied with the soft answer which turneth away wrath. We don't see what redress or hope for the suffering citizens the future can hold out. Pre- sumably traffic will continue increasing, and the bridge will go on swinging regardless of the fuming crowds on either side. The bridge has been swung 5,868 times this year, according to the Company's own estimate, and what a loss of time this has involved to the general public can be easily reckoned up by anyone with a taste for calculation. The average time of each stoppage of traffic -will be quite five minutes.</p>
        <p>Services for Sunday. At several of the churches on Sunday evening the services will be of a special festal character. At Wycliffe selections from Gaul's Prince of Poace " will be given by the choir, whilst at Bold-street Wesleyan Church the selections by the choir will be from "The Messiah." Bold-street Wesleyan Church : Preachers, 10.30, Rev. J. Manning ; 6.30, Rev. S. Pitt. Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church : Preachers, 10.30, Rev. W. Mellor ; 6.30, Rev. J. Manning. Wycliffe Congregational Church : Preacher, 10.30, Rev. H. Wall; 6.30, Mr. A. Corney. Golborne street Baptist Church : Preacher, Rev. 3. Ellison. Subjects, 10.30, "Life and its Compensa- tions" ; 6.30, " The Mother of Jesus." Latchford Baptist Church : Preacher, Rev. J. F. Matthews.' Subjects, 10.30, " Sir Oliver Lodge's Catechism " ; 6.0 "The Greatest of Heroes."</p>
        <p>Military Funeral. The remains of the late Lance-Corporal Houghton, 28, Orford-avenue, whose • death occarred last week end, were interred at the Cemetery on Thursday with full military honours. Houghton, who was very popular at the Barracks was originally a member of the Ist V. 8., P.W.V., South Lancashire Regiment, and during the late war volunteered and was accepted for active service in South Africa. At the conclusion of the campaign, and upon his ' return to England, he joined the South Lancashire line regiment. Early this year he was taken ill whilst at Fleetwozi ' and later, was discharged from the army as being medically unfit. At the funeral there was an excellent muster of the members of the South Lanca- shire Regiment, who,headed by the band of the depot, annder the command of Lieutenent Hutchinson, follovtd,the remains of ' their late comrade to their last resting place. At.the conclusion of the service the "Last Post " was sounded and volleys were fired over the grave by a party in charge of Sergeant Morton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>CRAWFORD'S</head>
        <p>CRAWFORD'S</p>
        <p>"ROYAL GINGER"</p>
        <p>BISCUITS.</p>
        <p>rkiE NEW TEA TABLE DELICACY.</p>
