      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>The Walker Fountain ha been 'doing its duty unremittingly during the past week. It seems there is plenty of water to spare at Appleton and the authorities have hit upon this happy plan of getting rid of some of it. .</p>
        <p>The action of the Lords in rejecting the Education Bill brings the secular solution of the difficulties to the forefront again. There is no other logical way out of the sectarian morass, and the sooaer the Government takes the bull by the horns the better. * ' The ceremony of " confirming " the School Attend- ance Committee is not to take place after all. Councillor Ellison has evidently borne in mind Mr. Jolley's little joke and on Monday he moved with particular emphasis, the confirmation of the ialitittes of the School Attendance Committee. ** * _</p>
        <p>The " Heathens " are no longer in the throes of a heated controversy on the question of the planting of trees in their midst. A spirit of give and take has been adopted by the planters and those who have been dubbed the " anti-planters," and compromises having been effected peace once more reigns supreme. * * "The Duke of Norfolk has assumed a specially mili taut attitude towards the democracy. He owns vas property in land and buillings, irotn which he derives over half a million pounds a year. The Duke of Norfolk never created or earned a penny of his enor- mous wealth."—" Labour Leader.'</p>
        <p>* * Amongst the Reins of a collection of schollboys "howlers" given in the "University Correspondence and College Magazine " is the following:—' Cabal" is a short name for the English Prime Miaister; ca stands for Campbell, and h.t for Bannerman, and the I at the end means that he is a Liberal.</p>
        <p>The marked brevity and dispatch with which the annual meeting of the Warrington Agricultural Society was conducted is to be co,nmended to other local bodies. Had it not been for the voting by ballot which occupied the greater part of the time, the meeting would probably have lasted less than half an hour. _ _ .</p>
        <p>We should not be surprised if the ranks of the local Volunteer Battalion were decimated by indigestion during the coming week. A large number of the men have won prizes, and the awards distributed last night consisted mainly of whisky and rabbits, which if taken together would make a rather unholy concoction.</p>
        <p>* * * At a Free Church meeting this week the Rev. Silvester Horne trounced the House of Lords severely for a full hour on the Education question, and the meeting cheered him ecstatically. Then when he sat down the Chairman announced the hymn, " Who is on the Lord's side ?" Yet TIO one seerqed to notice the comical inappropriateness of the hymn. *,* Sixteen move suffragettes have decided to spend Christmas in gaol. They tried to raid the House of Commons on two days, were arrested in the ordinary way, and declined to pay the fine for disorderly conduct. We shall have the criminal classes corn-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Index to Inside Pages</p>
        <p>Page. 2. Mr. E. J. Guest and the Rector. Compulsory Military Training. • "Written in Scarlet ", 3. Special Christmas Story 11. Fergus Hume. Stockton Heath News. Local Billiard Matches. 0. Volunteer Prize Distribution. Trades Council. Children's Corner. 7. Football. Should School Fees be Abolished. Savings Bank Meeting. Coming Events. The Grand Master of the Order of Oddfellows, Bro. Tout, will to-day visit Warrington, and the event will be made the occasion of a special demonstration by local members of the Order. At a meeting which will be held in the Winwick-street Co-operative Hall in the evening Bro. Tout will deliver an addres Additional interest will he lent to the proceedings s. by the appearance of Bro. Wilde, secretary of the Order. A musical programme will be submitted. An excellent attraction during the Christmas holidays will be afforded in the Parr Hall, where there will nightly be exhibitions of animated pictures. Amongst the reportoire is a seasonable animatoc.craph "Aladdin aind his lamp." Included in the programme are con- tributions by a powerful variety company and altogether the entertainment should prove a very popular one. Matinees are announced for Wednesday, Thursday and Saturday. To-night the members of the Warrington Musical Society produce Handel's grand oratorio "The Messiah,' in the Parr Hall. This great work' is ever popular and there is every prospect of a crowded attendance. The artistes will include Miss Gleeson White, soprano ; Miss Nellie Anderton, contralto ; Mr. Robert Radford, bass ; and Mr. Charles Sanders, tenor. The !annual re-union of Grammar SchOol "Old Boys " will take place on the 31st January, when a dinner will be held at the Lion Hotel. Xmas PRESENTS in large variety, see windows. J. S. GARLAND,SI to 55, Bridge et., Warrington.— Advt. New Magistrates. The names of a number of local gentlemen have been recommended to Sir Henry Fowler, Chancellor of the Duchy of Lancaster, for appointment to the Imagistracy, but no reply has yet been received. XMAS PRESENTS in large variety, see windows.— J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge st., Warrington.— Advt. Local Musical Success Miss Maud Jennings, 140, Wilderspool Causeway, has successfully obtained the Diploma Associate in Music. She has gained the maximum number Of marks (100) which entitles her to append the letters A.V.C.M. after her name, also to wear the College gown, hood and cap. General Buller's Visit. The date of General Bailer's visit to unveil the memorial to fallen soldiers to be erected in Queen's Gardens has not yet been definitely fixed. We under- stand, however, that the General has consented to come on a Thursday in February and three dates have been submitted to him. It is hoped, however, that the ceremony may be arranged for February 28th, which is the anniversary of the relief of Ladysmith. Burning Fatality. A verdict of "Accidental death " was returned at an inquest held at Warrington on Wednesday by Mr. F. A. Jones, deputy coroner, on the body of Edith Bramhall, aged five, of Lewis-street, who died on Sun- day from terrible burns about the body. On Friday last, during the absence of her mother, she upset a cup of tea on her pinafore, and whilst drying it in front of the fire her clothing became ignited. . Death. of a Roman Catholic Priest. Roman Catholics in Warrington and district will hear with regret of the death of the Rev. Francis Blake, Rector of St.. Lewis's Church, Croft, who passed away on Wednesday morning in his sixtieth year. The deceased came to Croft from Pemberton three years ago, and was much esteemed in the village. For a considerable time he had suffered from cancer. On Thursday evening a solemn dirge was sung in the church, the service being conducted by the Rev. Father Ryan, of, Warrington. At the funeral at Croft, yesterday, there was a large attendance of local priests and parishioners. The arrangements were in the hands of Messrs. Magian Bros. Warrington Technical Students Science Assoolation. - - - -</p>
        <p>A meeting of the students was held on Tuesday last at the Technical School,to form the ab we Society. It was decided that monthly meetings should be held for the reading of papers, and afterwards for discus- sions on any point of scientific interest to the studenjs. Besides meetings, visits will be paid t-) the various works in and around Wrrrington. It it was then decided to elect Mr. Robson as president, and Mr. Fisher, as chairman fop the ensuing twelve months, after which the other members of the Committee were appointed.</p>
        <p>- - PUBLIC BENEFIT.-UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—Advt. Distress Committee. . •</p>
        <p>The Warrington Distress Committee met at the Town Hall on Tuesday evening under the presidency of Alderman Tinnion. A-sum of £25 was alotted to the clerks who had carried on the work of the Com- mittee. It was resolved that the labour bureau be continued on. the present lines for a further six ' months. Councillor Stott expressed dissatisfaction with the limited powers of distress committeei.in providing employment and added that the question would have to be dealt with nationally before long. Alderman Tinnion was re-elected chairman of the Committee and Councillor Stott vice-chairman.</p>
        <p>Shorthand Writers' litsociation. A. social under the auspices of the above Associa- tion was held at the Winmarleigh Cafe on Wednesday when about 60 members and friends attended. The first part of the evening's programme consisted of a whist drive, which proved very interesting. Supper was then partaken of, and the remaining portion of the evening WOS devoted to music and dancing. Mr. Tom Broadhurst was at the piano, and 'Jr. H. A. Webb and Mr. H. Warburton rendered some capital songs. The duties of M.C. were ably carried out by Mr. Grounds, and much credit is due to Mr. A. N. Legge, who undertook the secretarial duties in connection with what was voted to have been a most enjoyable evening. Warrington Ragged School and Mission. On Sunday evening the limelight service at the above Mission was conducted by Mr. J. Owen. The story of " Jonah " was most interesting and instruc- tive. An illustrated hymn was sweetly sung by Miss J. Miller.—On Monday evening a capital entertain- ment was given by the Band of Hope workers. A service of song "Jack of the Ferry " and some humorous slides were shown, the connective readings being given by Mr. H. Wright, who also gave a fine rendition of Trouble in the Amen Corner," which was beautifully illustrated.—The accompanist was Mr. Blakemore. Mr. Albert G. Odell was the lanternist. Xmas PRESENTS in large variety, see windows.— J. S. GARLXKD, 51 to 55, Bridge at., Warrington.— Advt.</p>
      </div>
