      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>TRADES AND LABOUR COUNCIL. The Pauper Labour Question.</head>
        <p>TRADES AND LABOUR COUNCIL. The Pauper Labour Question.</p>
        <p>The question of the employment of pauper labour outside the Workhouse was again mentioned at the monthly meeting of the Trades and Labour Council held at Bold-street school on Tuesday evening under the presidency of Mr. T. Plumb. A letter was read from Mr. J. P. Awbery, tie master of the Workhouse, as follows :—I acknowledge the receipt of your letter which I will lay before my com- mittee at their next meeting, which will not be until the 30th inst. Permit me, however, in my private capacity to point out that what your Council require the Guardians to do" to investigate this case and con- demn such a system" has already been done by them. I assured my committee, as I do you, that apart from the recent incident no members of this Board have in my time ever availed themselves of pauper labour at cheaper rates than the prevailing standard, and the feeling of the Guardians in their last resolu- tion indicates that they would not permit or intention- ally condone any such practices, and I am confident that this is their true attitude. I venture to think that the recent incident was animated by motives other than to undersell the standard rate of wages, but be that as it may, your object will have been achieved by the sub- sequent development of events, for the Guardians have nothing further to investigate, because, as I have pointed out to you, this exploitation of pauper labour is as objectionable to them as it is to you, and that having regard to all the circumstances, its repetition is not calculated to occur in the future. After this explanation. I should be pleased to have pointed out to me what remains to be investigated. I again reiterate these are personal observations of my own uninfluenced by any motive, but with a desire to draw to its termina- tion an episode which should leave all of us the wiser for its occurrence. _</p>
        <p>It was decided to allow the matter to close. The engaging of Mr. Browne in the recent police court case was endorsed, as it was considered that he had acted in the best interests of the general body of trades unionists. Councillor Stott expressed the opinion that more had been done in the interests of trade unions by engaging a solicitor in such exceptional circumstances than by twelve months' agitation.</p>
        <p>CORPORAL PUNISHMENT IN SCHOOLS. On the motion of Mr. Coivaix, seconded by Mr. RANDALLS, a resolution in favour of the abolition of corporal punishment in schools was passed. SUGGESTED EVENING MEETINGS OF THR BOARD OF</p>
        <p>GUARDIANS. Mr. POOR moved a resolution asking that the meet- ings of the Board of Guardians be held in the evening to suit the convenience of woricmen representatives. Councillor STOTT seconded and Messrs. HALEY, RANDALLS and PODMORE supported and the resolution was passed unanimously.</p>
        <p>THE SCHOOL FEE QUESTION. A discussion took place on the decision of the Educa- tion Committee refusing to abolish school fees in all the schools of the town, in which the members of the Council strongly condemned the action of the com- mittee. Mr. Plinston and several other members pro- tested strongly against Mr. Fairclough's classiftcation of the scholars into desirables and undesirables. Eventually it was decided to let the matter drop, as the immediate object—that of having the Beamont schools free—was attained.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>Wscliffe P.S.A.</head>
        <p>Wscliffe P.S.A.</p>
        <p>There was a large attendance at the open meeting of the Wycliffe P.S.A. Society on Sunday when Mr. James W. Slack presided, and spoke upon the words "Always watchful. They must, he said, be watchful against evil and watchful to do the right, as it was easy to go wrong and to do wrong. It was always well to be on their guard, and to watch for opportunitiess to do good, to avoid profane language, bad temper, temper that destroyed the peace of home and its higher love. (Applause.) Mr. W. Beecher Smith of the Y.M.C.A., Manchester, spoke upon " Goodness, kindness, and thoughtfulness," which, he said, were far-reaching in their effects and never out of place. Messrs. R. Mills and George Wallington also took part in the meeting. Mrs. A. Booth sang "Rejoice greatly " and " Hear ye, Israel," delighting the meeting. Mr. W. H. ;Payton presided at the organ.</p>
        <p>The crew, 23 in number, of the Newcastle steamer I Elfreda, were landed in Dundee on Thursday night by the Glasgow steamer Glenlee, whieh sunk the Elfrida in a collision off Flamborough Head the previous day, The British Minister at Pekin has informed the Chinese Government that Britain will consider sym- pathetically any proposals which the Chinese Govern- ment may make regarding the importation and taxation of opium. Such proposals are now being considered by Britain.</p>
