      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>Examiner, Ssturda3T, December 22, 1906.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Ssturda3T, December 22, 1906.</p>
        <p>THE BEAMONT SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>EDUCATION COMMITTEE AND THE HEADMASTERSHIP.</p>
        <p>Lively Discussion.</p>
        <p>THE FATE OF LOCAL APPLICANTS.</p>
        <p>The sppointinent of the headmaster of the Beamont council School gave rise to a long discussion at the meeting of the Warrington Education Committee on Monday evening. As we announced last week, the managers of the school recommended the appointment of Mr. William Mason of Eccles as head teacher of the boys department at a commencing salary of £225 and Miss Mary E. Taylor, of Fairfield school, as head of the girls' department at a commencing salary of 'EI2O. At a meeting of the Scheel Management Committee, however, it was resolved, that the recommendation of the managers of the "Beamont " Council school be approved and adopted with the exception of that portion relating to tie appointment of Mr. W. Mason as headmaster of the boys department. Tbe Rev. Father WILSON formally moved and Mr. UesoN seconded the confirmation of the minutes. The Rev. CANON STEVENSON moved an ameniment that the report of the School Management Committee be adopted with the exception of the portion relating to the appointment•of Mr. Mason to the headmastership. He pointed out that there was a very large number of applications,—totalling nearly 200. The managers spent a very considerable time and took a great deal of trouble, and gave their impartial consideration to the applications. They proceeded on the principle of first of all eliminating those who from lack of the best qualifications or from being either too old or too young did not appear so desirable for the post. Having done that, they narrowed the applications down to twelve. They interviewed all those twelve—some of them more than once—and finally decided to appoint Mr. Mason. When the appointment came before the School Management Committee it was carried in a very small committee by a majority of two that the appointment should not be confirmed. The mover of the resolution moved it on the ground that Mr. Mason was not a local man, while the seconder apparently did not agree with that but took his action on the ground that the School I Management Committee and the Education Committee ought to exercise a very real control over the managers of tire Beamont School, and that they must be kept severely in order. (Laughter.) As to Mr. Mason not being a local man, the mover of the resolution, to whom they always listened with great respect because of his educational qualifications and because he represented the teachers of the town, laid great stress on that point as he always had done, and justly so. There seemed to be a common and mistaken idea that there was some bias in the minds of the school managers against the local teachers and that had been expressed in one of those happy aphorisms they got from time to time—"No local men need apply." He was perfectly cer- tain that nothing would have given the managers greater pleasure than to have been able to put the appointment into the bands of one of the teachers of the town. (Councillor WILKINSON : Hear, hear.) They had seventeen applications how Warring- ton teachers. When they proceeded on the process of elimination they laid down certain rules for their guidance, and one was that they should give the prefer- ence to a teacher who had gained a first class in his examination over one who only obtained is second class. It was a matter of opinion whether they were wise and right in making that rule, but it seemed to them that, all other things being equal, a first class man ought to be better than a second class, and it would be rather a strange thing to appoint a second class man as head of a new and important school. Having taken up that position, it ruled out fourteen of the seventeen local applications and left three. Carefully considering those three, they came to the conclusion that one of them, although a first chase man, was not particularly suitable for the kind of school they had in view. The remaining two were included in the selected twelve and one of them came down into the three last amongst whom the appointment lay. Another thing which guided the managers was that they felt they did not regard this as an ordinary appointment or as they might regard an appointment to some other school in the town, but as an appointment of very considerable importance. This was the first Council school that had been built in War- rington. It was to be a large and important school and would, so to speak, set the standard for the schools of the future. It would be very much under the public eve and would be subjected,no doubt, not to unfriendly but to very keen criticism. The managers had felt a very deep sense of responsibility in making the apppointment. After having ruled out the second class teachers, a frrther question came to their minds : Ought they not to select if possible some gentleman who had already shown that he could take the headship of an important school and conduct it with success? He thought it was that feeling which finally led them to rule out the last local applicant, who was an assist- ant, and to appoint Mr. Mason who had had previous experience in the headship of two important schools and had conducted them with remarkable success. There seemed to be a feeling that a great hardship had been inflicted on Warrington teachers, It was said that if the committee would not appoint an assistant to a headship in Werrington, the assistants were also debarred from obtaining headships in other places. He did not know on what ground that statement was made, but he did not think there was anything in it. He had had the happiness of being able to place several Warrington teachers as beads of various schools in the district. He would like to see something of this kind : that the Warrington assistant teachers should in course of time take first the headship of some school in the town which would not be of the great importance of the Beamont school and then those who proved themselves to be worthy heads should eventually be removed to a more important school. The managers of the Beamont School felt they were not able to find just the gentle- man they wanted amongst the local candidates—good as many of them were—whom they could safely place at the head of the school. As to the School Manage- ment Committee, having power to set the appoint- ment on one side, technically and legally that was perfectly right, but there was something to be said on the other side. At a previous meeting of the School Management Committee the managers brought forward certain minutes, including the advertisement for the appointment which did not stipulate a local man. It was then said : "Why should you after all the work you have done, bring forward these things for us? Why should we not treat the Beamont school managers as other managers are treated and merely ask them to submit their minutes for the sake of knowledge from time to time—to give them a free hand pra3ti- cally." The managers understood that was to be their position and that when they made the appointment they would have the same freedom of hand as other managers, and that no 6ne would quarrel with their appointment unless it were on educational grounds. When Mr. Mason was appointed the managers congratu- lated him. They made no reference whatever to any necessity for the appointment being confirmed by any one else, and they sketched and indicated to him certain things concerning his work and promised him their sympathy and support. No doubt Mr. Mason had received many congratulations from all his friends, and his post at Eccles was adver- tised as being vacant. This had placed the managers in a very painful position. If they were to be told one day that they were going to have 'a free hand and the next that the apoointment most be over-ruled they would find it difficult to get gentlemen to come forward and undert.ke the work. Mr. R. T. FAIRCLOUGHseeonded. H said Mr. Mason happened to be one of the four last applicants for the National School headmastership and he was a very able man. Irrespective of that it was the dnty of the com- mittee to support the managers who were appointed to do certain work, unless there were certain grave reasons why they should not be supported. Mr. A. J. JOLLEY said he should vote against the amendment, although one felt in a difficulty because what Canon Stevenson said about Mr. Mason's position was very forceful. Canon Stevenson had tried to gild the pill, but they could not get away from the fact that there was a very distinctly distasteful pill administered by the managers to the large body of teachers. He had no hesitation in saying that he could name half-a-dozen men in Warrington employed as assistants, any one of whom was, in his judgment, qualified to govern a new school and to govern it well. The difficulty of assist- ants obtaining headships under other authorities was increasing. As a matter of expediency the Committee ought to let it be known to the assistants that they were prepared to recognize good work, and to encourage it by promotion. That seemed to him to be a sound Principle. Mr. HENRY ROBERTS supported the amendment. There was, he contended, no fair objection to it, unless it was to be made on educational grounds pure and simple. No one had suggested that the best man had not been selected. If it was admitted that the com- mittee had to the best of their knowledge and judgment selected the best man. there was no case for the opposition. He agreed that there might he half-a-dozen a dozen, or even the whole-seventeen if they lilted, assistant teachers quali- fied to manage the school, but up to what standard? That was the point. If they were content to accept their present standard of management, all right. But they did not say that when they advertised. They advertised presumably for the best man they could get, and unless among the local applicants they had a man equal to the man they had selected, they had no right to go back on the advertisment. It would be unfair to those who applied and it would shut up the competition for the principal head teacherships practically to local men. If the committee restricted their appointments to local men, other authorities would do the same. _ .</p>
        <p>Mr. ITPQON : They are already doing. Mr. ROBERTS : If they are doing, they are not act- ing justly to the interests of their constituents and of till children. How many of the Warrington head- masters, continued Mr. Roberts, were Warrington men? They must not think he was decrying Warrington. He</p>
        <p>was a Warrington man himself and he was very proud of his own talents. (Laughter.) He thought the com- mittee had been approached too often by what, after all was said and done, wAs only a sectional interest, in • regard to the conduct of the administration of educational affairs in the town. He thought it had got once too often for the teachers of Warrington as a class pure and simple to put their interests against what the committee considered to be the interests of the community. Not long since the committee asked for what they thought was a necessary and a simple thing to be done, but the teachers practically declined to do , it and the committee gave way. The Beamont school managers had gone to the trouble of wading through 150 applications with a desire, if preference c-uld be given justly to give it to local applicants, but they had had to abandon the desire. Now their work was to be thrown away and the committee coerced into securing an appointment against the best man who, in the managers' judgment, had applied for the situation. He (Mr. Roberts) would be no party to any such thing. Mr. UPSoN said he did not know anyone who would impute motives or charge the managers, of the school with having any bias against the teachers of Warrington. They thought, however, that the managers had made a mistake. He admitted the difficulties of the position and the fact that the managers had put themselves into a false position. They had told Mr. Mason that he was appointed, but they had no power or right to do so. The appointment lay in the bands of the committee and not in the hands of any body of managers. His attitude on the question of appoint- . ments had been consistent during the four years he had been on that authority. He acknowledged that the voluntary managers of Warrington had not set them a good example, but he had urged that in appointments preference should be given to local candidates. The School Management Committee unanimously accepted a proposition in May asking the managers of the voluntary schools in making appointments to give pre- ference to teachers who were or who had been employed in the borough. He acknowledged that the position was painful for the managers and for the candidate they bad selected ; but he maintained that the balance of pain lay on the other side. They had seventeen local men, each of whom was competent in the eyes of the Government to take charge of a school, but vet they said that not one of them was good enough. The feel- ing among the teachers was exceedingly keen and bitter ; they thought they had no hope in Warrington. Canon Stevesson had said that teachers had been appointed from Warrington to headships of schools in other towns. They had not, however, been appointed to Council schools. Mr. ITpson instanced several large centres where promotion lists were kept and local teach- ers thus obtained benefit front their diligent services. While these towns shut out theWarrington candidate, Warrington admitted their candidates to come and take the good appointments. Mr.Upson pointed out that the action of the School Management Committee did not offer inducements to their own pupil teachers, upon whom they were spending a great deal, to take up posi- tions in the town when they became highly qualified. There were men in Warrington who were quite as highly qualified as the successful candidate but who did not apply because they thought the Committee would be aware of the qualifications of other men who had been • workieg in the borough for fifteen or twenty years with a magnificent record of services rendered in assistantships, and give them the preference. Hen who had University distinctions had refrained . from applying on those grounds. They did not think the question of a second class would debar the local applicants, because the difference between a first and a second was sometimes infinitesimal. As far as he could ' see from a brief resume of their quatifications,there were ' applicants who had higher University distinctions than • the successful man. If the committee were going to appoint on academic distinctions alone, why not appoint the best man with the highest distinctions—not reject a man because he was only a second class and select , another with lower University distinctions. Canon Stevenson had urged that education in the Berimont , school would suffer if the appointment were not con- . firmed.He Mr.(Upson)maintained that education in all the , schools of the borough would suffer if it were confirmed. • They had working in the schools a large body of men who ; felt they had been unjustly treated and they said their , chances of promotion were absolutely nil in Warrington. , He acknowledged the difficulty of the position but he I maintained that in the interests of education in thel managers should have selected the local man. Rev.Joms YONGB in supporting pointed out that therel , was no record on the minutes of such wide pourers I having been given to the managers as had been sugges- ted, and therefore the Committee bad a right to object t to the apnointment on any ground that was within reason. it was a pity that the teachers who were bear- ing the burden and heat of the day should be led to believe that they would not be considered when chances I of promotion arose. That was a manifestly unjust and I unhappy situation.lf the Committee's decision were not confirmed the discussion would have cleared the air,and probably there would in future be a general disposition to give every possible chance to the ladies and gentle- men who supported the educational work in the local schools* Alderman BOLTON said the managers had selected, in t their own view, the best man, and if the committee thought that only a local applicant should be appointed ' they should have given definite instructions on the point. Mr. PLTNSTON asked for proof that the managers had - selected the best man. Feeling was very rife on the I matter, for not only in this case but in filling up vacan- cies at the Secondary school and other high posi- tions not a single local man had been appointed. t Councillor BALL, as one of the managers, heartily . supported the amendment and said the managers had an honest and sincere desire to appoint a local man if it were at all possible. They could not, however, • appoint a man to the position simply because he was a local man. They were supposed to select the best applicant. They did not deny that there were a num- ber of capable men in Warrington who could take con- trol of the school, but their duty was to select the best man who came before them. With all due defer- ence to the local men they could not see that there was one better than Mr. Mason. In the towns where pro- motion lists were in force there/ had been a School Board system and the authority had therefore had the opportunity of watching its et-nolo:s'les and gradually ' promoting them. He ventured to Say that there was • not a member of the committtee who if he had seen the applications and interviewed the candidates would not have agreed that the successful one was better than any local man who came before them. Every consideration • was given to the matter, the qualifications being most carefully ..compared.</p>
        <p>ConnCalor lortn, another manager, said he was strongly in favour of a local man being appointed and fought for that up to the last. The managers were not unanimous in the appointment. One third fought for the post being given toe local applicant and he thought they had assistants in the town who were fully qualified to take the post. Now that the matter had been opened, he should certainly vote for the reversing of the managers' decision in which he was one of the minority, The managers were certainly advised that they had .the final appointment to make. - -</p>
        <p>Councillor WILKINsoy, also a manager, said that he arrived late at the managers' meeting and was told that they had unanimously decided to strike out second class applicants. it was surprising to have to listen to men telling the committee how kind their feelings were towards Warrington teachers when they were actually parties to a decision which was the means of "slaughter- ing " fourteen of the seventeen local men at one blow. Mr. Hands was the local applicant left in the final with Mr. Mason. Councillor Pemberton proposed Mr. Mason's apnointment and it was carried unanimously. The Rev. JAMIIS DANIES announced his intenfdon of supoorting the managers. believing that they were given full power to make the anpointment. ' Councillor LORD said Councillor Wilkinson had charged him with a change of attitude. He would leave it to atnon Stevenson to-say whether Cormcillor Pemberton and himself changed their attitude until they were forced to do so by the local men being thrown out.</p>
        <p>The CakIRM AN eaid that having advertised the posi- tion to the world. it implied that they would appoint on the merits of the applicants. Notwithstanding his great love for the town, he thought thA committee were bound as a matter of fair play to support the managers. Canon Stevenson rose to reply but was ruled out of order.</p>
        <p>The voting was follows FOR THE AMRNIYMENT. Aa UNST. Rev. Canon Stevenson Rev. John Yonge . James Davies Councillor Evans Alderman Bolton /I Barton Councillor Sankey II Dickenson II Wilkinson /I Lord II Ball Messrs. A..T. Jolley Toole „ E. Plinston If Ellison „ J. Upson—S. „ Miss Williams Messrs. R. T. Fairclough /I C. S. BargeWl \ ,0 G. H. Kingdon ~IT. Roberts CL Walker /I C. H. tinsworth—l6. The amendment was therefore carried and Mr. Mason's appointment is confirmed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>Christmas Tree at Stockton Heath.</head>
        <p>Christmas Tree at Stockton Heath.</p>
        <p>The annual Christmas tree and sale of work in connection with St. Thomas' Church were held in the Church Room, Stockton Heath, on Saturday afternoon, in aid of the Society for the Propagation of the Gospel in Foreign Parts, the Waifs and Strays Society, and for parochial expenses.—The Rev. J. Collins (vicar) presided.--Mrs. Frost, in opening the sale, said they had always had a good sale at Stockton Heath, and she hoped that this year they would have a record one. (Applause).—The Rev. H. A. Lester proposed a vote of thanks to Mrs. Frost.—Mr. T., R. Westbrook seconded, and the motion was heartily carried.—There were refreshment, plain work, fancy. Girls' Friendly Society, children's table, and Christmas stalls, as well as a Christmas tree, and bran pie.—The sale realized about £6O.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>VOLUNTEER PRIZE DISTRIBUTION.</head>
        <p>VOLUNTEER PRIZE DISTRIBUTION.</p>
        <p>The annual prize distribution in connection with the Warrington Volunteers took place at the Drill Hall last evening in the presence of a large gathering. Mrs. Edelsten, wife of the c)mmander of the battalion, handed the awards to the successful competitors. The following are the prize lists : WINNERS OF COMPANY CUPS.</p>
        <p>A COMPANY.—Challemge cup for general efficiency Sergeant G. Ryder, 105 points. B COMPANY.—CoIour-Sergeant W. Spencer, general efficiency cup ; Serteant F. Howard, shooting cup. D COMPANY.—Sergeant A. Woods. E COMPANY.—Sergeant T. %Vial tfield. F COMPANY.-Ist,-Sergeant J. Lloyd 178 points Lance-Sergeant E. Sankey 183 joints ; Recruits cup, J. Callan 108 points. CYCLIST COMPANY.—Private J. H. Spence, winner of cup and first medal ; Private Rathbone, winner of cup and second medal. APPLETON RIFLE MEETING.</p>
        <p>SERGEAK'TS.-A Sergeant Dr. J. W. Plinston, silver cup, value £2 ; H Sergeant D. Ridyard, la 10s. ; F Sergeant J. Lloyd, £1 ; B Sergeant F. Howard, 15s. ; T Colour-Sergeant T. Ashworth, 12s. 6d. ; A Quartermaster-Sergeant J . Wilcock, 10s. F Sergeant S. Moss, 7s. 6d. ; A Colour-Sergeant T. Jolley, ss. CORPORALS.-C Lance-Corporal G. Allen, £2 ; A Corporal C. Ashton, El 10s. ; Cyc. Lance-Corporal J. I Gleeson, ; M.I. Corporal W Furness, 155.; ' C Corporal T. Bossward, 12s. 6d. ; B Corporal R. Armstrong, las. C Lance-Corporal T. Daintith, 7s. 6d.; C Lance-Corporal W. F. Taylor,. ss. PRIVATES.-Cyc. Private H. Spence, £2 ; Cyc. Private R. Allen, .£1 lOs ; D Br.W. Miller, El ss. ; M.I. Private W. Matthews. £1 ; D Private C. Woods, 15s. ; M.I. Private J. Whalley, 12s. 6d. : D Private' J. T. Collins, 10s. ; H Private J. Thornton. Ss. 6d. •, D Private W. Dunn, 78. 6d. ; H Private P. Jefferson, 7s. ; II Private F. Wright, 6s. G. ; B Private J. Haslam, 6s. ! E Private E. Payton, ss. 6d. : E. Private W. Kennedy, 59. 6d. ; B Private J. Ravenscroft, ss. ' • Private A. Somers, 4s. 6d.; D Private J. Millett, 4s. (id. C Private; C. Da.rk, 4s. ; B Private W. senior, 4s. ; Cyclist A. Barber, 3s. 6d. ; C Private J. Pugh, 3s. 6d. ; E Private S. Dickenson, 3s. ; M.I. Private W. R. Smith, 3s. ; C Private T. Caswell, 3s. ; F Private J. Teece, t7e. BEST RECRUITS (Annual Course).-G Recruit J. Bate, 12s. 6d. ; H Recruit J. Price, 10s. ; 0 Recruit T. Keyes, 108. ; H Recruit H. Ashcroft, 10s. ; I Recruit J. Newns, 7s. 6d. ; D Recruit J. Shaw, 7s. 6d. Mi. Recruit H. Richardson, 7s. 6d. ; E Recruit T. Cotterill, ss. ; H Recruit G. Whittaker, ss. ; D Recruit J. Newton, 59. ; A Recruit T. Taylor, ss. ,• C Recruit 0. Owen, 3s. ; A Recruit J. Forbes, 3s. ; F Recruit J. Callan, 3s. ; A-Recruit T. Taylor, 3s. JUDGING DISTANCE.-F Sergeant J. Lloyd, £2; F Colour-Sergeant C. Russell, .C 1 10s. • A Sergeant. Thornley, £1 ; A Colour-Sergeant T. Jolley, 10S. SIGNALLERS' PRIZE.-C Private H. Jones, 21 ; F Private F. Foulkes, 17s. 6d. ; E Lance Corporal T. Sheath, 15s. ; C Private L. Condliffe, 7s. 6d. Sergeant ' C. Ashton, and Corporal T. Smith (Instructors) who did not compete in the competition, 10s. each. ' Signallers who attended 20 drills and attended camp. -Sergeant C. Ashton, Corporal T. Smith, Lance ICorporal Sherratt, Lance Corporal T. Sheath, Private A. Harris, Private T. Conroy, Private J. Battersby, Private L. Concliffe, Private F. Foulkes, Private T. Moolly, Private H. Roden, Private H. Jones, awarded 10s. each.</p>
        <p>SCOUTS PRIZES.—F Lance Corporal J. Chadwick, Li ss. ; M. I. Private L4'. Coakley, 17s. Gd. ; D Private R. Wicks, 15s. ; I Corporal J. Barker, 12s. 611 ; F Private E. Dower, 10s. ; I Lance Corporal J. Mannion, 7s. 6d. ; M.I. Private F. Hardman, 7s. Gd. ; B Private J. Itaveuscroft, sg.</p>
        <p>COMPANY FOOTBALL COMPETITION.—SiIyer medalswith name inscribed to the following members of the. M.I. Company—Sergeant T.Honghton,Private H.Richardson, Private a Hadfield, Corporal W. Farness, Private E. Hardman, Private C. Williams, Corporal P. Leitch, Private E. Buller), Private W. Cragg, Private R. Ratcliffe, Private J. Wells. CHRISTMAS PRIZE COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>Winners of prizes offered by local tradesmen are as follows :—Sergeant T. Richardson, Sergeant-Dr. J. W. Plinston, Sergeant W. Roberts, Sergeant J. Massey, Colour-Sergeant T.Jollev, Sergeant n Maginn, Corporal T. Clarke, Sergeant T. Campbell, Pte. F. Aiken, Bugler S. Catterall, Bugler W. Miller. Pte. J. Brirnelow, Lance-Corporal F. Allen, Sergeant F. Moss, Pte. T. Jackson, Pte. G. Knight, Colour-Sergeant W. Spencer, Pte. S. Knight, Colour-Sergeant J. Jones, Pte. G. Vale, Sergeant L. Robinson, Sergeant T. Connor. Lance- Corporal John Mather, Pte. J. Norman, Sergeant T. Ice, Sergeant R. Rigby, Pte. T. Potter, Pte. E. Dower. Lance-Corporal 0, Allen, Sergeant E. Woods, Pte. E. Parr, Pte. J. Taylor, Cblour-Sergeant T. Ashworth, Colour-Sergeant W. Woods, Pte. J. Henry, Sergeant G. Fogg, Sergeant T. Whitfield, Sergeant B. Howard, Sergeant C. Dagnall, Pte. H. J. Spence, Pte. P. Jefferson; Sergeant C. Thornley, Corporol W. Furness, Pte. F. flarrop, Pte. G. Heath, Pte. C. Whittaker, Pte. E. Cunliffe, Pte. H. Woolstoncroft, Sergeant 3. Lloyd Pte. T. Dunn, Pte. J. Caswell, Sergeant R. Chadwick, Pte. J. Pritchard, Corporal T. Lowe, Pte. 3. Haslam, Band-master I', Richardson, Sergeant J. Hesketh, Pte. M. Yeadon, Colour-Sergeant J. Batten, Pte. A. Alcock, Pte. J. Millett, Pte. G. Hansford, Pte. W. Cooke, Pte, T. Rutter, Pte. R. Gray, Sergeant E. Baskerville, Pte. J. Church. Pte. F. Hughes, Pte. J. Charnell, Pte. P. Hanglin, Sergeant H. Holloway, Pte. T. Buttrev. Sergeant J. L. Scott, Pte. E. Finch, Sergeant T. Houghton, Pte. .T. Gibbons, Sergeant W. Taylor, Pte. C. Armstrong, Pte. L. Mallett, Pte. C. Wolstencroft, Pte. S. Shipley, Pte. F. Gerrard, Pte. H. Watkins, Colour-Sergeant J. Thomas, Pte. W. Cooper, Pte. J. Langhorn, Pte. H. Charnock, Corporal R. Armstrong, Lance-Corporal W. Hill, Pte. H. Creamer, Sergeant H. Morris, Pte. F. Lloyd, Private P. Morriss, Private T. Isherwood, Pte. T. W. Payton, Pte. F. Dark, Pte. F. Hardman Pte. W. Higharn, Pte. H. Ashbrook, Pte. H. Wright, Pte. H. J. Smith, Pte. A. Barber, Corporal T. Bossward, Lance-Corporal J. Illidge, Pte. G. A. Condi iffe, Pte. J. Gibbons, Pte. T. J. Burnett, Pte. F. Coakley, Lance-Corporal T. Platt, Pte. G. Cole, Pte. T. Dai nteth, Pte. A. Stretford, Pte. G. W. Pendleton, Pte. J. Gleave, Pte. H. Titling, Pte. J. b'tnngleton, Pte. L. Sutton, Pte. J. Crozier, Pte. J. Massey, Sergeant C. Ashton., Pte. H. Rigby, Lance-Corporal W. P. Wright, Pte. .1. Inman Pte. C. Hardman, Pte. R. Allen, Pte. W. 'Grundy, Pte. W. Scott, Pte. J. Wakefield, Sergeant G. Bebbington, Pte. J. Smith, Pte. H. Read, Lance-Corporal J. Chadwick, Pte. J. Longworth, Pte. J. Whalley, Pte. J. Rigby. Corporal J. Stockton, Sergeant A. Hampson, Pte. J. Miller, Corporal H. Whitfield, Pte T Whalley, Pte. P. Donlan, Lance-Corporol Gaslin, F. Barry, Pte. B. Pearson, Sergeant A. F. Goulden, Corporal J. Barker, Pte. J. E. Newns, Pte. R. Steven, Pte. R. Norbury, Pte. J. Ravenscroft, Pte. J. Forbes, Pte. W. Kennedy, Pte. J. Robinson, Pte. A. Holmes, Pte. W. Davies, Pte. A. Caldwell, Pte. J . Crookes,Pte. S. Dark,Sergeant G.Ryder,Lance Corporal J. Lawton, Pte G. Pritchard, Pte. R. Burrows, Pte. P. Barber, Pte. J. Skelhorne, Pte. J. Gregory, Pte, G. Sankey, Pte. J. Newton, Pte. G. H. Jones, Lance Cor- poral Glover, Pte. J. Monks Pte. J. Thornley, Pte. T. Gill, Pte. W. Harris, Pte. Ve. Allen.</p>
        <p>Pte.</p>
        <p>Crosflelds' Recreation Club. There was a large concourse at Bank. Quay on Tuesday evening to witness this season's first display of the Girls Gymnasium Class. The spacious workmen's dining hall gave an ample arena with abundance of room all round for employes, relatiVes and friends, while the newly erected stage accommo- dated representatives of the staff and a number of visitors, among whom were Mrs. Roberts and Miss Roberts, Mrs. F. J. Lord, Miss Adams and Miss E. Birchall. The programme was performed under the direction of the Instructress, Miss Gertrude J. Beres- ford, N.S.P.E.. Miss Pooley doing duty at the piano, and comprised barbell drill, marching, German horse and pyramid, German drill and vaulting, advanced dumb-bell drill, parallel bars and tableaux, flag drill and a flag maze, concluding with a most effective tableau in which were massed all the members of the class, over sixty in number. It was a most admirable performance throughout, whether regard is had to the strength, agility and grace exhibited in the more strenuous individual exercises, or to the rhythmical precision of the combined evolutions in which the girls' simple costumes of dark blue and pink sashes made a charming picture. For anyone who doubts whether gyymnastics are fit and " proper " for girls we could imagine nothing better to resolve them than to contrast such a spectacle of lithe, strong-limbed girlhood, broad-chested, rosy-cheeked and bright-eyed with the wag, willowy, wasp-waisted, swan-necked type so dear to our ancestors who considered every- thing unfeminine that was not feeble. The girls all looked happy too, and after all thal is the main thing</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>CONTRIR</head>
        <p>CONTRIR</p>
        <p>es' . As this will be my last letter to y a beforel Christmas, I am taking the oppoe.unity of ' wishing you all the best and jollies ofsChrist- mases it is possible for you to har,:e, and in your turn I hope you'll all help much as possible any of those who are not s.. abundantly provided with this world's gootls is you your- selves 011elp them to understano and feel the true 6pinit of Peace and Good NHL By so doing, not only will you help oth .rs, but it will be the means of increasing your 4 wea happiness. I am sure there are many little mes who will have a far happier time that they ever ex- pected, thanks to the splendid c. utributions kg nice warm things sent in by on: members. I must say that the League has x ,sp'onded ex- ceeding well to this cause, and I trust that by ' the time Christmas Day tis here -I iall have a whole host of beautiful things for ristribution among needy little ones. Well, anly to think, in about three days' time we shall be welcom- ting the best day in alPthe three huno-ed and sixty-five—welcoming it with stockings pillow- cases, and other •handy things for re,eiving iianta Claus' acceptable gifts. Ah, me, how well I remember the (lays when I eagerly lung up the stocking, and tin. the morning sea lned its contents—books, oranges, ,nuts, sweets, toys (not clockwork, oh, dear, no, not in those d ys), and now—well, I beteve I .get just as much in in seeing others have their time. But I n qst plead guilty to one weakness, and that is: I do . enjoy sitting by the fire in the twilight of Christ. mas Day • eating delicious "hot rdast chestnuts, arid telling ghost stories—you know the kind, about "How in the loathsome vault beneath ,the haunted chamber the ghostly deed was coinmitted,"` 200 years ago," etc. Yes, I enjoy this. Give me a good story about ghosts, ogres, elves, vampires,,,etc., and, why, bless my soul, hey presto! I'm a younker again, and a youngster's time is generally 'the happiest. Well, boys, girls, and everyone, make the most of Christmas, 1906; have a romping, rollicking , time. Fill all your hearts with gladness and mirth, and your little insides withtsome tooth- some goodies, as plum puddings, mince pies, Fry's tempting chocolates, and Mason's fruity , Wine Essences, and when the holidays are things of the past, write hind tell me how you • enjoyed them, and I'll commence the New Year by Wing a lovely prize to the sender of the best contribution. May Santa Claus Pay you all a visit is the worst wish from • UNCLE Nun.</p>
        <p>-It is a very nice story, Alice, sorry I have no room this week. see if I have space next. tz</p>
        <p>:. I</p>
        <p>zse, ) be !less any old</p>
        <p>Teoly to your kind letter, Of cou. after one gets fourteen they cease to reckoned as children, but neverthel I am alwaya glad to welcome letter or contribution f rom an</p>
        <p>!ornerite.. o.—Was very pleased to get your kind `ter and thanks. .bo glad you enjoyed ,hoes.</p>
        <p>tk you for your story and good The same to you, old chap. . print your contrilmtion later sible. This week is reserved for sttnas. 'Take care of the minutes" ia very</p>
        <p>)d, and I will make use of it.</p>
        <p>WARD.—Many thanks for the very nice shawl you have sent. You did not put your full address; where is Wetmoor Road?</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Ned,—l am sending you a "Christmas fireside game" for the "Child- ten's Corner." It is entitled—</p>
        <p>"THE NEWS."</p>
        <p>This game Should afford plenty of fun. Each player represents some particular trade or pro- fession,, and, this being arranged, one member of the party is provided with a newspaper, from which he reads aloud an item of news. Suppose it is a paragraph relating to a wedding, and he reads as follows :—" On Thursday last a fashionable wedding took place at Kensing- ton. The bride, who was led to the altar by—" Here he pauses and glances at the butcher, who promptly puts in, "A sheep's head." "Was tastefully attired in "—glancing at the grocer, who unhesitatingly says, "A jar of treacle." "She also wore a superb--" Doctor: "Pill-box." "And a beautiful wreath of real—" ironmonger: " Fire-shovels." "While each bridesmaid carried an exquiffite bouquet of—" Greengrocer: "Carrots."' And so the game goes merrily on. With mitch love, I remain, Your loving niece, HILDA HOUNCE.</p>
        <p>'WINKING.</p>
        <p>Thi43 game is played by any number of people in this way. To take a number, say, six girls , are seated in a circle of seven chairs, ope of which is empfy. Behind each chair stands a gentleman. The one who is behind the empty chair winks at a girl he wants to occupy his 'chair, and she should try ..and rush across, and the gentleman behind the chair she is already on must try to prevent her. If he does not succeed he pays a forfeit, and then, as his chair is empty he must wink for another girl, and so on. After a while the ladies change places - with the gentlemen. Each time the person succeeds in reaching the empty chair they must kiss the one who stands behind it: ,</p>
        <p>"I'VE GOT A BASKET." This is a game where several plvers are seated round he room, and one starts by saying to the player at their left, " I've got a basket." The person.told must say, "What is in it?" and the person who has- the basket says "Apples," or something commencing with the letter A. X, 2 player asks No.. 3, and eo - ort all round, but each must name something beginning with "A." The second round com- mences with "B," and then all the alphabet is taken in rotation.</p>
        <p>Another good game for everybody is</p>
        <p>THE PERILOUS WALK. A certain number of players are seaied, say four or more a side, and aboot a yard and a quarter apart, so as to form an aisle between. Each player places an orange at their feet at a distance of about twelve inche,s, from that of the opposite player. This leaves a passge be- tween the VW° rovvig of oranges wide enough for a person to walk between. A person is then asked to ;walk down the centre blindfolded Without kicking the oranges. Of course, as soon as they start the oranges are all piCked up, but the person walking imagines he is avoiding them, as he touches-none. When he gets to the end of the walk the oranges are put down again, and the bandage taken off the walltr's'eyes. and he thinks he has succeeded when he looks ronnd, but he sees the joke when the next persorti tries to do it. , ,</p>
