      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>WELCOME TO</head>
        <p>WELCOME TO</p>
        <p>Alice Tubby, Mary Ireland, Beatrice Mantle, Sagar Haworth, John Shercliff, Arthur Sher- cliff, Grace Shercliff, Herbert Pilling, Jack Haworth, Annie Matthewmen, Doris Matthew- man, Nellie Dean, Dora Wood, Beatrice Fletton, and/ Dorothy Mille. -4--</p>
        <p>Have you ever tried asking a number !of people to make a mark against the wall the height they think a tall hat is? If not, do so, and you'll find that most of them will guess about twelve or more inches, whereas an ordi- nary silk hat when placed on its crown is only a little over six inches.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>RETIREMENT OF SANTA CLAUS.</head>
        <p>RETIREMENT OF SANTA CLAUS.</p>
        <p>Oh what becomes of Santa Claus When Christmas Day is over? Does he go back to Fairy Hall, Or is he ,still a rover?' And climbs he still o'er starlit roofs, Adown the chimneys pleeping? , And of his :visits leaves his proofs Where little tots are sleeping? In vain they hang their stockings now, And list for sign or token' Of stealthy footsteps, whispers low, .With midnight slumbers broken. . Perhaps he's met with some mishap— The thought itself is shocking— Or gone to take a twelve month's nap, In some tall fir-tree rocking. But don't you know he has a shop, Within the fairies' palace, And there for months to come he'll stop And work for you and Alice. And all deserving girls and boys, He surely will remember, And manufacture lots of toys, To bring them' next December. „ And don't you know he does repairs, And when Your things want mending, He fetches them unawares, When you are not attending. But, Oh I fear he shakes his head, y When all about him strewing He sees your ill-used dollies dead, And horses past renewing. And so, if thus you give him pain (Mind, this is just between us), Old Santa may not come again, But take his toys to Venus 1</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>TO FAIRYLAND.</head>
        <p>TO FAIRYLAND.</p>
        <p>•</p>
        <p>"Where are you going to, baby small? Where are you off to, eh?" " Straight to 4.1 e land where the fairies call Little boys 'off to play." "What shall I do in the fairies' land? What shall I play at there?" "Play in the castles we ,all have known, Halls that are built of air." "Run in the woods where the Christmas-tree. Bhissom, with lovely toys, Beautiful presents that fall to please Good little girls and boys." "Sail in an ark painted crimson-lake</p>
