      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <p>'</p>
        <p>APPLY TO DEALERS FOR BTJSSEY'S CATALOGUE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <p>NORTHERN RUGBY LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>RESULTS OF SATURDAY'S MATCHES. , G. T. P. ' G. T. I'. Warrington Dewsbury Halifax ' 3 1 9 Keighley Hull K.R 2 4 16 Withedeld T 0 3 9 Oldham 9 3 13 Leeds 0 0 0 Runcorn Rochdale H 1 4 14 St.Helens 1 2 8 Barrow 1 3 11 Salford Broughton R. Leigh 3 10 36, Liverpool City... 1 0 2 Warrington Avenged on Hunslet.</p>
        <p>A fine game was witnessed at Warrington on Saturday when Hunslet were the visitors and in the end victory rested with the home side by 16 points to 10. Fine weather prevailed and there was a crowd of about 4,000. 'The homesters were weekly represented, for such men as Fish, Isherwood, Taylor, Hockenhall were all absent from the backs, but capable substitutes were found in the " A" team, Jordan taking the left wing position and Cartwright right centre. Teams : Warrington—Berry, back; Brooks, Cartwright, Dickenson and Jordan, three-quarter backs • Lees and Jenkins, half-backs ' • Preston, Naylor, Jolley, Thomas, Harmer and Heath, forwards.</p>
        <p>Hunslet—Place, back Farrar, W. Goldthorpe, Eagers and Ward, three-quarters backs ; A. E. Gold- thorpe and Jacks4n, half backs ; Higson, Shooter, Nelson, Jukes, Brookes and Randall, forwards. Referee, Mr. T. IL Marshall, Bradford ; touch judges, Messrs. E. Pearson, Leigh and W. Mainwaring, Salford.</p>
        <p>- Warrington were the first to attack, and following brilliant work by Heath and Dickenson, Jordan had a chance in the first minute, but the three-quarter kicked too hard with practically a clear chance. Hunslet rallied, and after Eagers had mulled he made a. remark- able recovery, and punted well inside the Warrington quarters. A smart dash by Brooks Was ably net by Farrar, who saved on the extreme left. Warrington maintained the advantage, and after Heath had been pulled up near the line, Less opened out the game to Jenkins, who sent on to Brooks, who dashed over at the corner. Eurtalet responded splendidly, and, the forwards momentarily holding the advantage, Albert Goldthorpe tried to break through,Dicketison upsetting calculations near the line. but a moment later Jenkins was robbed of possession by Eagers, who raced through the backs in fine style, and Albert Goldthorpe placed a goal. Warrington rushed to the Hunslet end, and Dickinson securing possession made an opening for Jordan, and an easy try was scored. Preston again failed at goal. Hunslet then attacked again, and Albert Goldthorpe had a futile shot at goal frdm long range. Pursuing their aggressive work. Jackson crossed near the posts, for Albert Goldthorpe to place a \second goal {and give Hunslet the lead by ten points ‘to six when the interval was reached. I Turning round, the home forwards soon forced the pace, and dribbling effectively Place, Eagers, and Walter Goldthorpe were constantly defending. Eagers got his side out of a very tight corner, and, together with Farrar and Jackson, compelled the " wire- pullers " to fall back. The superior play of thef home 'vanguard, however, had its effect, and eventually Thomas charged the full back's kick and was credited with a try near the posts, Preston adling the goal. 13 ith Jordan and Heath were held up on the line, and in reply the Hunslet backs gave a true taste of their abilities, the three-quarters passing splendidly, when a strange whistle caused them to cease making progress. The incident caused great annoyance, and Mr. Marshall together with the Warrington officials, were some minutes trying to find out the offender. On resuming the visitors appeared to never get into their stride. Dickenson scored a brilliant try for Warrington lifter really fine passing. Preston added the goal. Result : —Warrington 2 goals 4 tries (In points), Hunslet 2 goals 2 tries 1;10 points).</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE COUBINATION. WIGAN HIGIIIIELD V. WARRINGTON "A."</p>
        <p>Watrington "A " Leans were unfortunately not at full strength on Saturday and had to submit to defeat on the Wigan ground. From a scrummage in midfield Healy set the Highfield backs going. and getting posses- sion in the Warrington quarters, Nl'Nicholl evaded Jones' and transferred to Forster who scored. Webster failed at goal. Warrington attacked, and after several packs Foster scored. Warrington had another fine chance to score, but failed. A rush by Highfleld ended in Silcock scoring, Forster failing at goal. Warring ton broke away, but Highfield attacked determinedly. and from a scrum in the centre the forwards dribbled away and Webster picked up and ran under the posts, Foster goa.ling. Warrington again passed finely, but Landor was upset by I\l`Cann. Interval score, High- field eleven points, Warington three, Warrington immediately took up the offensive, and Kay scored a try, Enigat goading. High- field eventually reached the Warrington quarter and Webster landed a goal from a penalty. linight kicked another goal for Warrington. Result : Highfield three tries two goals (13 points), Warrington two tries two goals (10 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION INTERNATIONAL</head>
        <p>NORTHERN UNION INTERNATIONAL</p>
        <p>The committee of the Northern Nnion on Tuesday night chose the teams for the match between England and other nationalities to be played at Leeds on January Ist as follows : England.--41. Gifford (Barrow) : S. Johnson (Leigh), R. Wilson (Oroughton Itangers), J..l.onuts (Salford), J. Leytharn (Wigan) J. Jolley (Runcorn), R. \Vard (Leeds) ; J. Bartle (Halifax). J. Be€ them (Broughton</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>, Answer in the Negative.</head>
        <p>, Answer in the Negative.</p>
        <p>The Education Committee, at their meeting on Mon- day evening spent a considerable time discussing the question of freeing the schools of the borough. The proposal came from the Finance Committee who recom- mended the total abolition of fees, which was, however, negatived. Alderman Bennett presided and there were also present :—Miss Williams, Alderman Tinnion, Bolton, Smethurst and Evans, Couvcillors Lord, Evans, Clegg,,Sankey, Barton, Ellison, Wilkinson, Dickenson, Fulton Smith, 'Ball, Toole and Craik, Revs. Canon , Morley Stevenson, J. Davies, N. Wilson and J. Tongs, Messrs. A. J. Jolley, C. S. Burgess, R. T. Fairclough, Walker, Cr. H. Kingdon, H. Roberts, J. Upson, C. H. Unsworth, and C. E. Parker, J. Moore Murray (director of education), R. B. Roberts (assistant director) and J. Fairhurst (borough treasurer). At the outset a little formal business was transacted. Mr. Reuben Higham was appointed a foundation manager of St. Peter's school and Miss Williams was appointed a representative manager of the Heathside National School. The Finance Committee reported that the Board of Education had intimated that they could not alter their decision declining to consent to the charging of fees at the Beamont Council school. It was resolved (a) That this committee regret the decision of the Board of Education, since it will render the charging of fees in other schools henceforth inequitable, and will necessi- tate the abolition of fees throughout the town and a consequent heavy addition to the burden of the rates.; and (b) that it be recommended that the Local Authority no longer sanction the charging of fees in any of the public .elementary schools of the Borough. . . . .</p>
        <p>On the question the Finance Committee presented the following memorandum :—The fees paid in he Elemen- tary schools in the Borough during the last financial year amounted to £2,473 las. sd. Of this amount £6lB ns. Id. was tranferred to the managers of the respective fee-paying schools, in accordance with Section 14 of the Act of 1902, for the purpose of assist- ing the managers to defray expenses incurred in the maintenance or improvement of the school premises. It is clear that the additional burden cast upon the managers by the withdrawal of the proportion of the fees hitherto paid to them will involve the crippling or destruction of many of the voluntary schools. If this at the outset only affected one-third of the non-provided school accommodation, and even if it were possible to make favourable terms with the trustees in whom the various premises are vested, it Auld necessitate a heavy expenditure on the upkeep and improvement of the premises, and make the total loss to the ratepayers much I heavier than the mere amount of the fees hitherto levied. This addition to the heavy burden of the rate- payer is a necessary result of the decision of the Board of Education, for, apart from any other considerations, the presbnce of a first-clogs modern school without fees in the midst of a group of fee-paying schools would make it impossible to contidue to obtainftes from the majority of parents of scholars attending such schools without resorting to legal proceedings which would leave behind , them a well founded feeling of resentment against both the managers of the schools and the members of the ' local authority. Mr. A. J. J&amp;LEY in moving the confirmation of the minutes, said that he regretted the decision of the Local Government Board from an educational stand- point. He mentioned that the Local Government Board had been approached and asked to take the view, which, they had taken, by the Trades Council and the Independent Labour Party, and said that whilst he had no quarrel with either of those organizations and respected their principles and the sincerity of their convictions he regretted that their opposition to the original proposal of the committee had been soccessful as he thought it was mistaken and harmful. The additional cost of primary education in the town, by reason of the abolition of the fees would be 2d. in the at the outset and an increasing amount in the future. That increased cost he feared was going to cripple and interfere with the proper development of the evening, technical and secondary schools, and of the committee's scholarship scheme, but under the circumstances it appeared to him that they would have to accept the decision of the Board of Eeucation and also the con- sequences of the decision. Councillor WILKINSON seconded, and in doing so ' took the line that it was not fair to make certain parents pay school pence that at the same time call upon them through the rates to pay towards the educa- tiod of other children. Mr. IL T. PAIRCLOUGFI moved the rejection of the minute. His first reason was that the increase in the rates which the proposal involued would be an extreme hardship upon workingmen and poorer shopkeepers. His second reason was that it was against the interests of education. It would be a very great mistake to do anything which would cripple the managers of the schOols in their efforts to make alterations which were desired by the „committee. Another great objection was on account of the parents themselves. There were in the town two classes of parents—desirable and tualesir- able—and they would, always find that the desirable parents would rather pay fees and have there children • associate with a certain class. Alderman BOLTON seconded the amendment. Because the Board of Education had done one injustice it was no reason why the Committee should do another. (Hear, he r.) 7 r. C.II. UNSWORTII supported the araendment.There another alternative which the Committee should give a trial. Surely some scheme could be devised whereby certain schools cotfld be declared free and others allowed to charge fees. That would tend to raise the tone of the schools in which fees were charged and would, he thought, work harmoniously. Alderman TINNION supported the minute. It was unfortunate that they could not test the opinion of the ratepayers on that question as it would have been desirable if some member of the committee could have retired and fought an election on it. They would have to face the question shortly and he did not think the Education department would much longer allow the charging of the fees. Another fault with the Education system was that the charge was a local and not an imperial one. He though the Committee were wise in facing the question hefore they had an agitation in the town for the freeing of the schools. If the proposal meant an increase in the rates it would make people take a greater interest in education. Mr. UPSON said he was irk favour of the abolition of fees but he thought the Finance Committee had been rather hasty. The persons concerned primarily were the managers of the schoal and the minute would come to them almost as a bolt from the blue. Their only scource of income would be taken away and it would mean the hastening forward of the time when all volun- tary schools would become a charge on the rates, as the managers would not be able to maintain them, and the cost to the town would be increased. In the matter he eounselled a little delay. INThy should they abolish fees all over the town merely because the Board of Education had declared one new school to be free. Continuing, and raking People's College as an example. Mr. Upson argued that the people who sent their child- ren to school paid the fees not for a superior education but for class. exclusion—a thing which they ought to pay for. Canon MORLEY STEVENSON supported the amendment They must look further ahead than the immediate cost to the rates. New schools bad to be erected imme- diately and they must not shut their eyes to the fact that several of the schools in the town were in such a condition that they would not be sanctioned much longer by the Board of Education and would have to be replaced by new schools. The minute was too drastic and there was no reason because the charging of fees in one school was refused why they should,without further consideration, abolish the whole of the fees in the , town, especially as there were already free schools. The Rev. N. WILSON said the freeing of the Catholic schools had resulted in those schools becoming to some little extent the dumping ground of undesirables. Ile did not see why they should free all the schools in the town. There was some difficulty) in regard to the admittance of children to the new school as they did not deeire it to be the dumping ground of those children to whom they had alluded. but he thought that surely sone scheme could be formulated to overcome the difficulty. The Rev. j. D also supported the amendment. Mr. PLINSTON supported the minutes on the ground that it would abolish class distinction. There were no such things as desirables and midesirables. • Mr. H. ROBERTS said that he was in entirely in favour of free education, and he did `not.see how, injustice. they could make a provided school free and at the same sime insist upon fees being paid in other schools. There was, however, a serious difficulty and he did not see how the managers of the '1 oltint (try schools were going to mate rip the money which they would lose by the with- drawal of the fees. Mr. Roberts thought that the Committee should wait until the present Government had dealt with the question of education. He also objected to the proposal on the ground of expense and urged that the Board of Edue4tion should he requested to receive a deputation on the question of the charging of fees in the Ileamont School. Councillor lini.r, in supporting the minute prefaced his speech with the remark that he did not . think the action of the Labour Party had had much effect upon the decision of the Board of Education as the same course had been adopted in regard to new schools all over the country. He did not think the managers. of</p>
        <p>L Mt Illicras</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, December 22, 1906.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="4" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>Increasing Prosperity of the</head>
        <p>Increasing Prosperity of the</p>
        <p>Warrington Savings Bank.</p>
        <p>IThe increasing Prosperity of the Warrington Savings Bank formed the subject of general congratulations at the 89th anual meeting in connection with the institu- tion which was held at the Bank on Tuesday. In the absence of Mr. W. Long (chairman), who was confined to the house with a cold, the proceedings were presided over by Mr. John Geddes (vice-chairmen). There were also present : Messrs. J. Hepherd, C. Jackson Holmes and Charles White (trustees), Revs. W. Bracecamp, C. Harvey-Cook, J. H. G. Hollingworth, G. Vale Owen, B. Percival, H. V. Pigot, Canon Wilis and Messrs. J. Longland, C. E. Parker, C. D. Parkinson, H.Smethurst, 'John Taylor and G. F. F. White (manager), and W. A. Proud (actury). In moving the adeptly of the financial statement,the CHAIRMAN said it was a great pleasure to find that the bank was going on in a satisfactory manner. Although the increase was not very large it was on the right side, especially in their ' special investment department, which had only been opened during the last twelve months. That department was going on very satis- factorily and was increasing. There had been an increase in the number of accounts of 589. The branch banks were making. very satisfactory progress, the one at Bank Quay showing an increase for ten months of £l,BOO, and that at Earlestown an increase of £2,000. He moved the adoption of the financial statement. The Rev. Canon ‘VILLIS seconded, and the motion was carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>REPORT.</head>
        <p>THE REPORT.</p>
        <p>The Actuary read the report of the Committee of Management on the business transacted during the year. In the Ordinary Department, 911 accounts had been I opened or renewed, and 490 closed, leaving 14,850 open accounts ; in the Special Investment Department, 175 accounts had been opened and 7 closed, leaving 168 accounts ; together a grand total of 15,018 open accounts. At the 20th November, 1905, there was due to depositors, £370,283 19s. The following is a summary of the accounts :- Ordinary Department.-Received from depositors, £83,511 its. 7d. ; dividends and interest paid and credited, £8,798 3s. 3d. ; total, £92,312 14s. Iod. The repayments amounted to £92,490 18s. 3d., so there was a decrease of £l7B 3s. sd. Stook Departments.-Purchases and transfers, £3OO ; sold £B3O ; a decrease of £530. Special Investment Department.-Received from depositors. £20,910 10s. ; interest • paid and credited, £377 13s. Bd. • total, £21,318 35. Sd. The repayments totalled £1,21 9s. ; or an increase of £20,106 14s. Bd. There was a net increase for the year of £19,398 us 3d., and the amount due to depositors on the 20th November last was £389,682 10s. 3d. The assets of the bank were as follows :-Invested with the Commissioners on general account, £350,375 16s. 2d. ; invested with the surplus funds accounts, £4,500 ; Government stock held for depositors, £17,322 Os. 2d. ; • mortgages secured by assessments, £19,000 ; interest accrued thereon to date, £237 19s. 9d. ; balance in treasurer's hands, £4,264 ss. 6d. ; freehold premises and furniture, £1,300 ; a total of £397,000 is. 7d. There is due to depositors on ordinary department, £352,253 15s. 5d ; on special investment department, £20,106 14s. Bd. ; and on Government stock, £17,322 Os. 2d. ; a total of £389,682 10s. 3d. ; leaving a surplus after paving every depositor in full of £7.317 Its. 4d., a net increase for the year, after paying all current expenses and the cost of altering and furnishing the Green-street branch of £126 Is. Id.</p>
        <p>The branches were making steady progress ; thus proving of great convenience to the depositors by enabling them to transact their business without much loss of time.</p>
        <p>The special investment department by its success had fully justified its inception, over £20,000 having been received or transferred during the year. When it became better known that this department offered a safe investment at slightly increased rate of interest, the committee anticipated it would be largely taken advantage of by those depositors, whose accounts had reached the limit allowed in the ordinary department. In reviewing the work of the past year it was most gratifying to the committee to have such an excellent report to present to the trustees and managers. Although there was a small decrease in the ordinary department, this was more than counterbalanced by the progress made in the new department. There was a net increase of £10,398 11s. 3d. in the amount due to depositors, which brought the total funds to £397,000 is. 7d. The turnover for the year was £198,602 Is. 10d. entailing 31,911 transactions. The continued steady increase in the number of accounts and number of trans- actions was a highly satisfactory feature in the work of the bank, the large proportion of which was of small amounts. .</p>
        <p>The committee regretted having to record the deaths of Messrs. R. W. Rownson, R. Dobson, and Joseph .11 ughes, managers for a number of years.</p>
        <p>THE COMPETITION OF OTHER BANKS.</p>
        <p>The Rev. H. V. Ptucrr moved the adoption of the report, and acknowledged the valuable services rendered to the institution by those gentlemen acting on the committee.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. LI. SmimfurtsT seconded, and considered the Bunk this year bad done remarkably well in face of the competition of other banks which were now offering similar facilities to the small depositors. He congratu- lated the committee of management on the success attained. The motion was carried. Mr. J. LONGLANI) responded on behalf of the Com- mittee of Management, and said the work throughout the year had been done very well, the accounts haying received the fullest consideration. ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>On the motion of Mr. CHARLES WHITE, Seconded by Mr. JAMES 111,PHERD, Mr. W. Long w.s re-appointed .chairman, and Mr. Geddess, vice-chairman. Eulogistic references were made to the great. interest 1 which Mr. Long has taken in the institution for many years. On the motion of Mr. C.,4ACESON HOLMES, seconded by the hey. .1. H. G. Hor.r.m; WORTH; the following managers were elected to join the trustees on the Com- mittee of Management :—Rev.John Yonge and Messrs. T. B. Carter, J. Longland, L. W. Reynolds, and J. S. 11111. It was also decided on the proposition of the CHAIR- MAN, seconded by Mr. C. JACKSON HOLMES. that the following be elected additional managers of the institu- tion :—The Rev. T. 3'. Lee-Jones, Rev. J. Collins, Councillor Steel, and Messrs. L. R. Rowsoli, F. Black- well, P. It. Pierpoint, J. W. Hayward, F. J. Lord and A. Down. The Chairman .was thanked for presiding on the proposition of Mr. .1. TAVLOE, seconded by Mr. II SMETHURST.</p>
      </div>
