        <p>MEADOWCROFT. The funeral of the late Mr. John Meadowcroft, of Hoole Bank, Chester, (who died at his residence on Saturday morning), took place at Upton (Chester) Parish Church, on Wednesday afternoon. Deceased I was clerk to the Upper Mersey Navigation Commis- ' sioners,and for 25 years manager of the Runcorn Docks. The service was conducted by the Rev. W. Sparling,and the mourners present were Messrs. John Meadowcroft Stanley Houlker Meadowcroft (sons), Messrs. Herring, ton and Houlker Meadowcroft (grandsons), Elsie- Gladys, Queenie, and Babs (granddaughters), Messrs James and Thomas Meadowcroft (brothers), Mr. John Oldfleld, (saperintendent, Bridgewater Department, Manchester), Mr. J. C. Wilson (snperintendent, Ship Canal, Manchester). Mr. H. Boddington (managing director, Anderton Company, Stoke-on-Trent), Captain William Yates (superintendent of the Lighting and Buoying of the Upper Merfey), Mr. Albert Draper (assistant clerk, Upper Mersey Navigation Commissioners), Mr. James Pritchard (representing the Runcorn Urban District Council), Mr. Whittridge (Shopshire Union Railway and Canal Company, Ellesmere Port), Mr. E. Pitchford (representing Mr. Thomas Hales, manager. Shropshire Union Railway and Canal Company, Chester), Mr. H. G Hope (solicitor), Mr. F. J.Finlay (Bridgewater Depart- ment, Preston Brook), Messrs. George Burrows, T. H. Priestley, and John Groom (office staff, Runcorn). Mr. Robert Rawlinson (formerly superintendent engineer), Mr. J. Evans (Ship Canal Company), Nlr. W. Shaw (of the Runcorn Seamen's Institute), S:c. Wreaths were were received from the widow, sons, grandchildren, Mr. J. R. Jebb (chairman of the Upper Mersey Navigation Commissioners), and the office staff of the hte Mr. Meadowcroft at Runcorn.</p>
        <p>Efforts are now in progress for trying to get the child into a horne,as the collier's mother says they are unable to keep it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON •</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON •</p>
        <p>INFANT SCHOOL CLOSED.</p>
        <p>The infant department of St. Peter's School was last Thursday again closed for three weeks owing to prevalence of whooping cough.</p>
        <p>THE LATE MR. JOHN EVANS.</p>
        <p>The will of the late 11Ir. John Evans, of Lethbridge- road, Southport, for 40 years a partner in the firm of Evans and Mantua+, general drapers, Wigan, and for many years in bnsiness in Earlestown, has been proved. He died October 4, and loft £6,574. DEATH AND FUNERAL OF' MR. JOSEPH AUSTIN</p>
        <p>On Wednesday last week the death took place, under very sad circumstances, of Mr. Joseph Austin, of Earlestown, at the age of 44 years. Mr. Austin, who was in business with his brother as corn factors and provision merchants in Legh-street, came to Earles- town from Atherton (Cheshire) about 17 years ago. He was of a quiet retiring disposition, very attentive to busine.s, and acted as Secretary to the Primitive Methodist Trust at Earlestown and was the teacher of an important class in the Sunday School. A few days ago he scratched his hand with a rough piece of metal, but it was so slight that he paid no attention to it until it began to give trouble. Dr. Valentine was called in,and so alarming had the blood poisoning which was set up become that twice specialists were called in, but their aid was unavailing. The deceased left a widow and one son. The funeral took place at two o'clock on Satur- day, a service being held at the Primitive Methodist Chapel.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>BACHELORS' BALL.</head>
