      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS. Increased Subscription to the Infirmary</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS. Increased Subscription to the Infirmary</p>
        <p>IA special meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians was held at the Workhouse yesterday morn- ing, when there were present :—Mr. J. Billinge (in the chair), Mrs. Clay, Mrs. Wilkinson. Miss Broadbent, Dr. Patterson, Messrs. 4 Brooks, A. J. Jolley, W. Hickman, G. Thompson, G. plathor, S. Davies, M. W. Seton, R. Bolton, W. Flarsliagan, J. C. Sutton (clerk), J. F. Awbery (master), A. Wright (superintendent of the Padgate Schools). In regard to an application from Mr. W. Peter Rylands for a subscription towards the extension of the Infirmary, the CLERK reported as follows : —At the last meeting of your Board, I was instructed to consider the power of the Guardians to contribute to the fund for the extension of the Warrington Infirmary, and the authority of the Local Government to sanction the same. Of course the exercise of authority in both cases 'is limited by Statute, and I cm find no precedent for the gift out o public rates in aid of a fund for the erection of a hospital maintained by voluntary contributions. Neither does there appear to be any statutary authority in that behalf. There is legal power to ' sanction an annual subscription towards the maintenance of volun- tary hospitals and it is with the sanction of the Local Government Board, the Guardians contribute out of the public rates .£lO 10s. Innually in aid of the main- tenance of the Warringtot Infirmary. Undoubtedly the medical and other relief of sick and of injured poor afforded at the Warrington Infirmary considerably relieves the union rates and extension thereof will acid to such relief. I therefore advise your Board to pass a resolution to the following effect : " That as the relief of sick and injured poor at the Warrington Infirmary relieves the union rates and as the enlarge- ment of the hospital at the cost of many thousands df pounds will further relieve the union rates, the local Government Board be requested to sanction an increase for one year of the amount of the subscription of .£lO 10s.' Mr. MATHER moved, in the terms suggested, that the subscription be increased to £lOO, and spoke in high terms of the work done at the Infirmary. Mr. THOMPSON seconded. Having been a manager of the Infirmary for 15 years he could testify to the good work done b 3 that institution for the poor.He hoped the Local Government Board would sanction the increase, as it was as little as they could do. Mr. A. J. JOLLEY said that if the proposal failed to secure the approval of the Local Government Board it would be open for the Board to show their appreciation of the work at the Infirmary by increasing their annual subscription. Mr. HICKMAN and the CHAIRMAN also supported the resolution, which was unanimously approved. It was announced that the Bishop of Liverpool had consented to conduct a service at the Workhouse some time in February next.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>Ruri-Decanal Conference and Education.</head>
        <p>Ruri-Decanal Conference and Education.</p>
        <p>The annual conference of clergy and lay representa- tives of the Winwick Rural Deanery was held at Warrington on Thursday, the Rev. Canon Penrhyn (Rural Dean), presiding.—The Rev. E. C. E. Carletoa and Mr. E. Draper were re-elected secretary and treasurer tespectively. The usual representatives on Diocesan Committees were appointed.—The Rural Dean appealed for support on behalf of the Bishop of Liverpool's fund, the object of which is to raise £lOO,OOO to secure sites for Churches and to support the existing diocesan institutions. A discussion took place with reference to foreign missionary work, and there was a strong opinion that there should be more !united effoCt. The Rev. E. C. E. Carleton moved : That the members of this Conference desire to record their sense of deepest gratitude to the Arch- bishop of Canterbury and Lord Lansdowne and their supporters in both Houses of Parliament for their defence, of the highest interests of the Christian religion and firm adherence to its principles during the course of the discussion in Parliament on the Education proposals of the Government, and to assure them of their continued and loyal support."—The Rev. James Davies seconded. He expressed his pleasure that the ,Bill was dead, and said he hoped it was decently buried, and that it would not appear again unless it wAs after an appeal to the country.— The Rev. J. Carson (Newton) said they must recognize that Nonconformists were their bitter enemies, and , that they had got great power. Churchpeople should show fight and keep their views before the public, because they must not think they were safe yet. Their opponents would continue in their endeavours so long as they had their present majority in the House of Commons.—The resolution was carried.</p>
        <p>The decision of Mr. Justice Bray that barristers must place receipt stamps on their vouchers brings about a complete revolution in the practice of the Bar. Counsel have never attached stamps to the receipts they have 'given for money received.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>PEOPLE'S COLLEGE CONCERT. ,</head>
        <p>PEOPLE'S COLLEGE CONCERT. ,</p>
        <p>SUCCESSFUL EFFORT FOR THE</p>
        <p>INFIRMARY.</p>
        <p>Once more we have to congratulate the teachers and scholars of People's College, one of our most honoured educational institutions; upon the great success of their annual concert. The delighted audience which filled the parr Hall to its utmost capacity on Thursday evening was' striking testimony to the fact that the school concert has now come to be regarded as one of the happiest events of the Christmas season. But on this occasion there was an even more powerful Teason• why a full measure of public support should be accorded. It was a happy inspiration which prompted the managers to devote the whole of the proceeds to the Infirmary extension fund, for such a noble object must appeal to every section of the community. With a. big undertaking like the extension scheme in hand, donations were never more urgently needed than at present, and the concert thus served the dual purposa of enabling the public to spend an extremely enjoyable evening and at the same time to lend some aid to a most deserving cause.</p>
        <p>The enthusiasm with which the revival of People's I College concerts was greeted several years ago, has in no way diminished and each succeeding entertain- ment has added another laurel to a proud record of successes. There was, however,a consensus of opinion that Thursday's concert was the best of the series— which is certainly saying a great deal. What par- ticularly pleased the audience was the great variety of the programme, and interest was not allowed to flag fora moment. Host praiseworthy was the precision which marked the whole proceedings and showed with what great care the children had been trained. To the strains of the organ, the boys and girls marched on to the platform and when they had taken their places a charming scene was presented. The effect was heightened when later the performers in fancy costume—beefeaters, life boatmen, fishwives and other's—added dashes of colour to the gay assembly. The stirring chorus " Ye mariners of England " formed a fitting opening item and it was followed by the fine national song "The brave old oak" which went with a capital swing. The choruses throughout the evening were given with taste and expression. Specially effective were "Down the Vale "—a beautiful melody feelingly sung—and the sparkling "A May morning." It was somewhat of a novelty to hear the rollicking old English chorus "Come, lasses and lads," which was interpreted in such a sprightly way as to call forth the warmest applause. Conceived in the humorous vein, "A capital ship" quite took tht fancy of the audience, and an encore was accorded. As indicating how highly appreciated the entertainment was, it is only necessary to state that every item apart from the choruses elicited a hearty recall. A group of girls in picturesque fishwives' costumes sang "Our Jack," a typical song in praise of the sailor lads. Then the audience was invited to con- jure up visions of the old-fashioned winter which has not yet arrived, a number of girls appearing in dainty dresses with red hoods and white muffs, and voicing the praises of Christmas time in " A song of winter." They made a very pretty picture and the actions, which must have been well studied, were particularly appropriate. Samuel Wilson, strikingly. costumed as a grandee, sweetly interpreted "Long ago 111 Alcala " which with its subtle humour and pointed moral, proved popular. One of the best features of the evening spectacularly as well as vocally was the well known number from "The Yeomen of the Guard " entitled " When our gallant Norman foes." The boys to whom this item was entrustad were clad in the old-time costumes of the beefeaters and carried halberds. They sang admirably of the grim old Tower of London and the tale which it could tell of the events of byegone days, and the somewhat wierd chorus was given with full effect. Meritorious in the highest degree was the solo " Sing me to sleep" by F. W. Sirncock. He possesses a sweet voice, betrayed no sign of nervousness, and fully entered into the spirit of this beautiful composition. Considerable interest centred in the item which closed the -first part of the programme. "The Lifeboat," as it had been written by Mr. C. H. Hands, second master at People's College, and com- posed by Mr. %V. H. Payton, Mus. Bac., the singing master. It was presented by n contingent of boys garbed in the sea-faring outfits of the heroes of our coasts, with cork belts complete, and the perform- ance left nothing to be desired. The song was rendered with fine spirit and at the close of each verse the boys, by imitating the rowers bending to the oars, were able to bring home the excitements of the lifeboatman's noble calling. It was gratifying to note an entirely new departure this year in the shape of selections— " Festal March " and " Pizzicato Gavotte "--bs members of the boys' and girls' violin classes. It was evident that this recently-inaugurated branch of instruction is making great progress and the selections were well received. The comical " Dolls' Duet " by Ada Peacock and Richard Atkinson quite took the house by storm. The youthful artistes were thoroughly at home in their parts and the amusing mechanical move- ments of the living dolls were so excellently simulated as to provoke it smile from the most serious individual. A merry quartette of sailor boys next tripped on to the platform and sane of "Four jolly sailor-men." It was quite like a whiff from the sea, singing and actions being in true nautical style. The skill with which the boys danced a hornpipe at the close roused a storm of enthusiasm, and two repetitions had to be con- ceded. The sailors had been trained by Miss Gertrude Brown, of Stockton,. Heath. Richard Lawton figured, not for the if time, as the humorist of the evening, and scored an instant success with Killecrankie." He was confidence Personified—a budding Harry Lauder in fact—and as the cEminutive figure in Highland dress strutted on to the platform armed with a formidable walking stick and very miniature bagpipes, there were roars pf laughter. The song was given with a humour which seemed natural, and when this little entertainer obliged with a couple of anecdotes with a rare Scottish flavour, the amusement of his hearers knew no bounds. Another laughable song was "The runaway musketeer," by., Arthur Warburton. and as the gallant soldier who neveri failed to decamp at the prospect of danger, be cut a ludicrous figure. He is an unusually sweet singer and the item was highly creditable. Still more variety was added by a delightful little selection "Eight little school-girls." The performers were tastefully gowned in blue, with poke bonnets, and looked pictures of demureness. A stately dance was gracefully executed and the pleasure of the onlookers found unmistakahle expression in cordial applause. The girls having made their exit the stage was next occupied by the Coleville Coon Cadets who with coai black faces and hands and white uniforms looked particularly striking. Their movements were characterised by military precision and they sang with equal accept- ability. William Barlow interpreted the popular " Bombay "in a creditable manner, although the high pitch of the song placed him at a disadvantage. Mr. W. H. Payton, Mus. Bac., was at his usual post of organist and accompanist and discharged the duties with his customary taste and skill. To him and to all the teachers who had devoted their time to the arduous work of preparation, the warmest praise is due. They were no doubt sufficiently rewarded by the happy consummation of their efforts in the shape of the signal success of the entertainment. During an interval Mr. J. RYMER YOUNG, one of the school managers, thanked the audience for their attendance, assuring them that such magnificent gather- ings did much to strengthen the hands of the teachers and the efforts of the scholars. With regard to the object, the extension of the Inflrmal-y bad overwhelm- ing claims upon their consideration hod upon their pockets, and it must be a source of satisfaction to the audience to know that by attending in such large num- bers they would assist the managers to hand over what be hoped would be a distinctly satisfactory sum. (Applause.) He thanked the parents'iwhose help had enabled them to place upon the stage one of the prettiest pictures ever seen in Warrington. (Applause.) They saw with regret from time to time in the news- papers that there were some bad parents in Warrington, but that gathering of healthy well-dressed children was evidence that they had crowds of admirable parents who understood their duties to their off-spring. With regard to the preparations for the concert, he wished particu- larly to thank Miss Brown, who had a great deal to do with the designindesigning'of the costumes and the actions and who was ably assisted by Miss Bayley. They were also much indebted to Mr. Payton. When Mr. Pattison, who led the concerts years ago, retired they were anxious as tb what would take place. Happily the mantle of Elijah had fallen with singular fittingness on Mr. Payton who had proved himself a worthy successor. (Applause.) He had also to thank Miss Wallington, Mr. Knowles, and in particular Mr. Bowen who was mainly responsible for the comic element. Mr. Wallington's interest in that magnificent school son. tinned unabated and he worked with unflagging zeal. He , was to-day what be had been for many years—an ideal headmaster of a popular school. (Applause.) That being so, it was not surprising that the institution continued in an unbroken series of successes, mainly owing to the splendid staff so capably managed by Mr. Wallington. (Applause.) Although the institution bad, 1,400 or , 1,500 scholars, representing every denomination in Warrington, never once during his (Mr. Voting's) twenty years' experience and, Mr. Wellington informed him, during the twenty years before that, bad what was commonly called the religious difficulty ever arisen in their midst. (Applause).</p>
        <p>The concert concluded with the singing of the National Anthem.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR</head>
        <p>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS. • Authors of letters intended for publication must in all cases send their names and addresses to the Editor as a guarantee of good faith.</p>
        <p>Meetings for Men. Sir,—Now the first series of the above meetings, con- ducted by the Rev. John F. Matthews is over I should just like to say how very enjoyable arid instructive they have been to those able to attend. The topics brought forward have been immediately concerned with our everyday problems, and have, as such, supplied a long- felt want in our midst. Mr. Matthews intends holding another series of meetings for men, early in the New Year (of which due notice will be given) and it is to be earnestly hoped the men of Warrington, young and old, will make a point of attending and thus helping forward a movement which contains the seeds of great and far reaching influences for good amongst them. That there, is need for the creation of an organised reformative, healthy spirit amongst the manhood of the town is patent to everyone having eyes to see. Mr. Matthews seems willing to lend every aid to such a movement and if the responsive spirit only gives the necessary encouragement nothing will, or can,prevent this moral force wielding its power and impressing its influence in quarters which obviously require it.—Yours &amp;c., H. F. Latchford.</p>
        <p>Beamont School Headmastership. Sir,—l must, as a Warrington teacher, dal a lid tire enter my protest against Mr. Roberts' unfair remarks at the Education Committee's meeting last Monday, as to the capabilities or otherwise of those unfortunate fourteen who were taken out from the seventeen local applicants for the headmastership of the Beamont Council School. We are told by a previous speaker that their names were deleted simply because they failed to pass as" First Class " in the Certificate examination, and that their qualification as teachers and organisers was consequently not discussed in the least by the Managers of the school. Mr. Roberts, however,says that none of these men are capable of conducting a school with the requisite skill as teachers and organizers, though nobody inquired into those points, and they became branded as "incom- petents" as teachers when their applications were put out of court on an entirely different issue. In some cases these men have taught in the town for over ten years, some nearer twenty, while the examina- tion they were cast on one side for, was passed from twelve to twenty years ago. The years of service are not allowed to count in the least degree, according to the manager's procedure, while Mr. Roberts says these men can only conduct schools of" ordinary standard " in Warrington. This is distinctly hard lines for "ordinary" Warrington schools, and it looks very like a rap all round. Our consolation must be that Mr. Roberts only judges our competence by our callow student days, and thinks that Warrington neither can teach nor has taught us anything. And after all this way of reason- ing is very Warringtonian. I admit most readily the necessity of taking into consideration of all examina- tion results, and this more especially in the Case of strangers to the committees ; but I contend, sir, that teachers who have worked, and worked hard in the town, have a decided right to have that work examined by committees appointing teachers, even though they may perhaps through no fault of their own, .be not quite in the sacred" First class." - Now let us see visiat this First Class "is. Years ago all certificates issued to teachers were called "Second "on issne, and these were raised to " First Class " by years of service,as certified by the inspectors of the Board of Education. (But you see this Com- mittee of Managers ignored" service " by the fourteen absolutely, even where they had every chance of tracing it.) Next, the results of the certificate examination were issued as First. Second and Third Class, the two first of which have always been reckoned as equal in the . opinion of the Board of Education in London. Later again, the certificate examination was divided into two parts, so that one individual might be, and actually is in many cases both " First ' and " Second." Then finally, to make the class distinctior more complicated still, the Board of Education have now done away with all class division, and are placing the Certificate candidates in the " Pass "and 'Fail ' divisions simply. Therefore, given all these diverse systems (and I could easily puzzle you further), I say that the first class man is the man who has provel his worth by actual , teaching, though he be not in the first flight at such a see-saw examination. Ido not say, sir, that the selec- tion of Mr. Mason is not the best possible, for I have no power of judging. and I for one trust that he will have a hearty welcome when he comes ; and I hope he will stop long enough with us to become a" local." But I do think that the managers ought to have given a little more discussion to the actual teaching done by our local men (because it could have been traced so , easily). And I think also that a most uncalled for slur has been cast on the whole of the town when it is said that these unlucky fourteen were only good enough to be appointed—" if they (the Committee, I presume) were content to accept the present standard of manage- mpnt." This is decidedly" nasty," and is not, I hope, a correct statement of the appreciation by the Educa- tion Committee of our work in the ordinary schools of the town.—l am sir, yours truly, CHAS. TURNER. Fearnhead, Warrington. December 19th, 1906.</p>
        <p>Non-Trading. Mr. C. E. Parker's Last Word. REPLY TO MR. CHRIMES. In a letter which reached us too late for last week's issue, Mr. W. Chrimes writes : Sir,—Mr. Parker invites the opinion of the people of Warrington on the general question. The invitation is characteristic. He is fond of generalities and is at home amongst platitudes. But has he such a poor opinion of the people of Warrington to think that after years of agitation and investigation they are as undecided as he is ? Does he think that after so many wards have been conteated and won, mainly upon Non-Trading issues, the working men of Warrington don't under- stand the rights and wrongs of the question as well as he does? Mr. Parker has apparently only considetred one side of the question yet, and that is the outstdl3. The ethical stage of the controversy which Mr. Parker is now ponderously trying to grapple with was passed years ago by scores of business men in Warrington who looked into things seriously. There is no questionabout the ethics at all. The least intelli- gent ratepayer in Warrington knows that it is not right to use the advantages and opportunities of a public posi- tion to further private and personal interests. Not does it require any large brain power to realize that It a councillor or an alderman is trying to make a good bargain with the Corporation, he cannot at the same time be studying the interests and saving the pockets of the ratepayers. In short Mr. Parker is simply begging the question. But I can assure him we have not the smallest intention of giving it away. Nor shall we allow him, after such a long and strenoous fight, to put obstacles in the path of reform. Mr. Dodds has asked NTr.Parker several plain,straight- forward questions, which have a direct bearing upon the position and attitude taken up by Mr. Parker himself at the beginning of the correspondence, and Mr. Dodds is entitled to have such questions answered. If Mr. Parker has no authority to bind the Liberal Party to any definite line of action let him say so, especially as he declared so loftily that "In all things doubtful there should be liberty." .flas his own rule become unworlc- able so soon? Does l‘lr. Parker expect political support from Non-traders, without giving or conceding any- thing in return ? He puts his own construction on the policy ane contentions of the Non-traders, and tries to show that under certain circumstances the traders of Warrington would be placed at a disadvantage. This was one of the misrepresentations so unscrupu- lously circulated in Latchford. But what are the facts np to date? Can Mr. Parker or anyone else give instances and particulars of the volume of Warrington trade being vtiminished by the action of the Non-Traders? Is it not a fact that, whilst a few firms may have lost, many have gained by their action? Have they not always advocated a more equal and impartial distribu- tion of the Corporation contracts ? Is Mr. Parker not aware that prior to the agitation it was almost impos- sible for some firms to get Corporation work and that on this point there is still much to be desired in the way of reform ? Why is a policy of secrecy so persist- ently followee ? Why are many councillors so ignorant of matters which they vote upon? Why does Mr. Parker not give Non-Traders credit for their efforts in the cause of economy and better management of the town's affairs? If he has followed their actions closely, he must know that they have saved his own pocket considerably. Jndaing from his letters, one would imagine Mr. Parker had been abroad a few years, and that someone had been telling him a prejudiced and one-sided story about a strange body of men known as Non-Traders. But if Mr. Parker is really conversant with the facts, if he thinks he alone is competent to set things right, let him meet me or any other Non-Trader who may be chosen, in public debate, on any platform in Warrington. As an alternative let him come with his "more excellent ways "to our next Parr Hall meeting. I have no doubt be will get a fair bearing and no one will thank him snore than the Non-Traders if he can show them that they have been labouring under delusions and that everything in cennection with the town's affairs is as it should be. If Mr. Parker can only give us substantial grounds for believing that a working majority of the Council, is now seriously °noosed to extravagance and determined to stop irregularities he will he welcomed. We have absolutely no evideuce that there is in the Council a general desire to reform, and until such evidence is forthcoming the Non-Traders will, I hope, carry out, without fear or favour, the duty they have been elected to perform.—Yours &amp;c., W. CHRIMES. CO, Knutsford-road, Latchford.</p>
      </div>
