      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>Penketh, 19th December, 1906.</head>
        <p>Penketh, 19th December, 1906.</p>
        <p>The Homelessness of the Poor.</p>
        <p>Sir,—The letter of I‘lr. C. E. Parker on" The home- lessness of the poor"is a presentation of one aspect only of the greatest r ocial evil,that of poverty, for the want of a decent home must spring from the same cause as the want of other material advantages i.e. lack of opportunity to secure a fair share of the produce of industry. It is no doubt true that the evil effects of this lack of opportunity are greatly aggravated by the motal shortcomings of those affected and that intemper- ance plays no small part in deepening the corruption land perpetuating the vitiated environment of the slum- dwellers.</p>
        <p>Mr. Parker gives us no definite suggestion for dealing with this complicated evil but if the expression " our poor must be given a fair nhance " indludes the oppor- tunity of earning an adevate living I feel sure he is on the right road to a solution because he will agree with me that the economic aspect of the problem is the key to the situation. You can safely abolish slum property if it is made possible for the slum-dwellers to earn sufficient to pay for better-class houses ; but to merely turn the people out of the slum property into a better class without otherwise altering their condition would simply result in reducing the better class of house in time to the condition of slum property. I have seen evidence of this. It seems, however, only natural to premise that there would be a spontaneous tendency to secure better houses when earnings warranted it and that compulsory measures need only be applied to that unfortunate part of the community whose environment hal stiflled every aspiration for self-improvement. Then as to intemperance which many people believe to be the sole cause of sloth conditions but which Mr. Parker has come to recognise as "interlocked with many other social difficulties," can it be doubted that, with a raising of the standard of living and the open- ing out of counteracting avenues of usefulness and pleasure consequent on increased earning power, the evil of intemperance would naturally be thrown off ! • It certainly does not appear strange to me that slums either in Warrington or elsewhere should be the product of the existing brutal system of industry. What does appear strange is that in this enlightened age so many people should seek a solution of the slum problem by dealing with such subsidiary questions as housing and temperance leaving untouched the main question of the individual's earnings. In any other rank of Society when a man wishes to improve his housing accomoda- tion the first question is one of finance, if he can afford it he may take a house in a better locality. Why the I slum-dwellers should be treated on different lines I fail to see. I believe the truest charity is to give them RS their right the opportunity to earn their improvements. Mr. Parker is quite right in drawing attention to the immense amount of " indifferentism " that exists, and it is lamentable that so many working men have so little regard for their own and their fellow‘' interests that they will sell their votes for beer. The greater culprits in this matter, however, I believe to be those who buy the votes who in most cases are looked upon as anything but" indifferent" to the workers' welfare. This" indifferentism " however, with the other ugly I accompaniments of a vicious industrial system will, think, vanish gradually when we have dealt effectually with the economic question on which all others impinge. It means a modification of the system from the very start. —Yours &amp;c., JOHN A. J. MORTON, President "Right to Labour League. Warrington, 21st December 190 G.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ' ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ' ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL. [By " (Wall it."]</p>
        <p>Notwithstanding the weak team they were compelled to place in the field on Saturday, Warrington succeeded in defeating a powerful Hanslet side by 16 points to .10, ex4ctly reversing the figures at Hunslet a few weeks ago. The game was. exceedingly vell contested, and though Warrington opened the scor- ing Hunslet twice took the lead and it was only in the closing stages that they had tn admit the home team's superiority. Hunslet have never yet won at Warrington but they came vere near doing the trick on Saturday, and they did not fail for want of trying.</p>
        <p>Warrington were seriously handicapped by the absence of such stars of the back division as Fish, Isherwood, Taylor, and Hockenhall and it says much for the capability of the reserves that they filled in the gap so creditably. Cartwright and Jordan both did well at threequarter, the latter showing a ! praiseworthy amount of dash and deter- mittation. If he could only knock a couple of seconds off his time for the " hundred " he would be very nearly a first-class wing. The greatest deficiency in the Warringto,n reserves to day is pace. In fact there is not a great deal of the real article in the premier team once Fish is taken away. It is unfor- tunate that the really capable " A " team three- quarters are not a little speedier. A sprinter would be a treasure on the wing, for the supply of centres is ample and good.</p>
        <p>I wonder how many more appearances at Warring- ton Albert Goldthorpe will make. He has been a prominent figure in the game for more years than he probably cares to count, and is still holding his own in the best company. There is probably not his equal as a goal-kicker in 'England to-day, and on Saturday he popped a couple over in the easiest possible manner. His fellow-half, Jackson, was perhaps the better of the two,. but there is no discounting " Albert's " value to the team. He is very much what the rudder is, to the ship. It is rather curious that Hunslet had the strongest team they have turned out this season, and they rather fancied their chance against Warrington's somewhat deple- ted forces. But as one of their officials said, Lancashire is a fatal spot for them, and Warrington a real burying ground. The visitors started rather weakly, but once Brooks had opened the scoring they seemed to find their feet. Eagers,the most erratic of players, suddenly came to the fore with a brilliant individual effort which brought five points. Warrington dashed off t the other end and Jordan secured the lead from Dickenson's pass. Back came Hunslet with clever passing and from a scrum near the line Jackson ran over in a way which made the Warrington defence look rather feeble. The goal followed as a matter of course and the -locals found themselves behind at the interval 10-6 o</p>
        <p>They resumed with a businesslike rush and in a brace of shakes had got ahead again. ,Thomas charged the full back's kick, secured the ball and passed to Preston. The latter ran under the posts but was pulled down, Thomas dropping on the ball in the nick of time. This time Warrington got the goal and were on velvet so to Speak. Hunslet several times caused anxiety but their play fell off considerably while Warrington improved. Near the end Dickenson, settled the issue by taking .rt re-pass from Jordan and raeing over cleverly near the posts, Preston again goaling with ease.</p>
        <p>The star of the home team was unquestionably Dickenson, who played magnificently from start to finish. He was always a souroe of danger to the Hanslet men, and on the defence proved invaluable. Just lately he has been playing better than ever in his life. Jenkins had his first serious trial at half-back and gave a very smart display. As a connecting link between the resourceful Lees and the threequarters he was i!apital and more than once showed how to beat a man and make an open- ing. Brooks' try came largely from the cleverness of - half-back. A word of credit is due to the ms who coped most successfully with the bulky Yorkshiremen. Heath was in fine form, and in the open he and his fellows quite outclasssed the Harslet six. Neither full-back was really at his best. Berry is not displaying his early season form but I quite expect it will come again. His one fault is an over- deliberateness which prevents him turning defence into an attack, and that is what he should do under the present rules.</p>
        <p>Ltihe new nr wards</p>
        <p>There was an incident during the match which was greatly to be regretted. Once when fiunslet were in possession a whistle clearly sounded from the " popular " side and the players stopped. Of course the offender could not be detected, but the affair created an unpleasant sensation. It was not quite so had as the case at Broughton last season when Warrington were the victims, however. On that occasion, Sam. James did not stop when the whistle sounded and the Warrington men let him get a try under the posts. It was not the referee's whistle, but Sammy never stopped to think. Perhaps he has such a fine ear that he can tell one whistle from another in the heat of the fray. Hunslet on Saturday did not suffer to such a serious extent, however, but they might have clone.</p>
        <p>1 There was another unpleasant feature of the match, and that was the unmannerly booing from the mem- bers' stand wheneYer the referee's decisions were not liked. Some of the " hqwling dervishes " seem to have got out of place. Then there is the bad practice of shouting and hooting at the man who is trying to place a goal for the opposing side. It may succeed in putting him off his shot, but it is rotten sportsman- ship on the part of the crowd, an I see it is Very properly condemned, by a writer in the " Leeds Mercury." Warrington will lose its good reputation in sporting circles if this sort of thing is allowed to continue.</p>
        <p>The following is the League Table np-to-,date : For Agst.</p>
        <p>Per. P. W. L. D. G. T. P. G. T. P.Centnec. Runcorn l4 12 2 0 46 72 308 16 18 86 85.71 Halifax l4 11 2 1 55 49 257 17 23 103 82.14 Leeds l4 11 3 0 46 49 239 18 27 117 7857 Broughton l4 10 3 1 45 59 267 15 20 90 75.00 Keighley l3 9 3 1 38 48 220 23 24 118 73.07 Oldham l6 11 04 1 32 46 202 25 24 122 71.50 Warrington 15 10 4 1 50 63 310 24 23 117 7000 Hunslet l6 11 5 0 49 66 296 34 41 191 88.75 Wigan 15 10 5 0 44 61 271 26 21 115 66•66 Batley l2 7 4 1 31 33 161 22 30 134 62.50 Barrow l4 7 6 1 27 37 165 32 42 190 53.57 Salford 15 8 7 0 30 35 165 37 39 191 53.33 Dewsbury l5 7 7 1 32 45 199 32 50 214 5000 Pontefract 9 4 5 0 13 16 74 23 37 157 44.44 York l2 5 7 0 21 29 141 26 40 172 4116 Leigh l5 6 8 1 25 52 206 28 33 155 43'33 Hull K. 11. _l4 6 9 0 22 37 155 26 31 145 42.85 Rochdale l4 6 8 0 22 38 148 19 37 149 42.85 Widnes ll 4 8 1 23 19 103 23 39 183 40.90 Swinton l5 5 6 0 33 48 204 35 39 187 40.00 Hull l4 5 9 0 20 39 157 43• 51 239 3511 Vlak'fild T l4 4 9 1 17 32 130 34 67 269 3214 Hud'sfleld l5 510 0 28 48 200 49 92 284 33.33 Bradford „At 4 9 1 31 43 191 30 38 174 3214 St. Helens 14 311 0 24 29 135 37 54 236 21.42 Bromley l5 Fl 3 1 714 58 42 74 non 10.00 Liverpl.C 17 016 1 10 15 65 82 149 611 2'91</p>
        <p>Not much confidence will be felt in Warrington's ability to win at Leeds to-day; but there are more unlikely things.. Isherwood's return to the team should make considerable difference on the attack and give the rest of the men more conadence. Bar Fish, the team is about as strong a one as the club could command, and if they Inly show their proper form on Yorkshire soil to-day the Warrington men should have a fair chance of recording their first win across the border.</p>
        <p>The selection is as, follows: Berry, full back; Brofts, Isherwood, Dickenson and Jordan, three- quarters ;. Lees and Jenkins, half-backs; Thomas, Boardman, Preston, Naylor, Heath and Harmer, forwards.</p>
        <p>Ike Taylor is having a-further,rest, a bad kick on the leg having given him some trouble. By the way there are good hopes of Warrington signing on a new full-back with a reputation second to none in Lanca- shire. The much-needed wing has not vet been found.</p>
        <p>Tuesday (Christmas Day) Leigh make their annual visit and should furnish more points, while the 1 following day Liverpool City should make their bow .... before the Warrington crowd. On the Saturday, the 7 team goes to Bradford, and on the following Tuesday ' (New Year's Day) Runcorn are due at Wilderspool. This should be the match of the season, and I fully , expect to see a spoke put in Runcorn's wheel. On that day Jolley and Thomas will be playing in.the Northern Union " international " game, the teams for which were picked this week' and are printed on another page. ;</p>
        <p>Footballers all over the North will regret to hear that poor Harry Myers of Keighley has passed away. On November 3rd he collided with a Dewsbury player and fractured his spine and has since been partially paralysed. Death ensued on Wednesday evening and closed a distinguished career. Myers had played for 13 seasons, and before the formation of the Northern Union he was " capped " against Ireland., He played for Yorkshire over a score of times, and also for the North v. South.</p>
        <p>Hockey. The girls hockey team belonging to the Technical School have so far had a most successful sesson, having won all matches played and scored 29 goals to two Among others they have defeated W igan Technical School, Leigh School Girls, the Sankey Ladies Team and Stockton Heath Ladies Team.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>WARRINGTON AGRICULTURAL SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AGRICULTURAL SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL MEETING.</p>
        <p>•</p>
        <p>Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart., presided over the annual meeting of the Warrington and District Agricultural Society which was held in the Mrusonio Hall, Bold- street, on Wednesday afternoon. There was a good attendance which included Mr. H. Bereeford Melville, secretary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-12-22_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>FINANCIAL.</head>
        <p>FINANCIAL.</p>
        <p>The statement of accounts showed an income of £747 35., and an expenditure of £927 3s. 4d., leaving a deficit of £l3O Os. 4d. To meet this Sir Gilbert Greenall made a special donation of £lBO, which added to the balance in hand in 1905, left a credit balance of £24 14s. 10d,</p>
        <p>ANNUAL REPORT. In his annual report Mr. Melville said :—The seven- teenth Annual Show was held in Walton Park, near Warrington, on Thursday, August 16th. With the record entries and the additional attraction of the Flower Show, the Council had hoped that this year would have made a change in the finances of the Society. Whether August must now be reckoned as a settled wet month or not, the weather was disastrous in every way. The Flower Show, which was confined to cottage tenants and workpeople on the Walton Estates with the exception of a few clasSes for gentlemen's gardeners, was fairly well supported ; the entries were not large out the exhibits sent were most satisfactory, and it is hoped if a flower show is held another year that the classes will be, better filled. The whole of the prizes were given by Lady Greenall, who has done, and is doing much good in the interest she takeg in promoting the cultivation' of flowers, fruit. and vegetables by cottagers. Some of g,srflens in the 'villiages of Hatton and Walton are well kept, and there should be no reason now why every cottage garden in the neigh- bourhood should not have something to show if there is space to grow it. Every encouragement is offered and there should be keen competition for the prizes for well cultivated gordens. The Walton show is now recognised as a great 'collecting place for shires,hunters, and hackneys, and the entries again gave evidence of this in the numbers and quality of the exhibits. It was once said that the Warrington and District Agricultural Show was one of ths best local shows in the country. but it is now considered that no show, whether county or local, can claim precedence for either numbers or quality over the shows held at Walton A third of the Council retire and are not eligible for re-election for twelve months. The names of sixteen members of the Society are submitted for the election of eight of the number thereof,to take the place of those retiring. The entry fees are not large, so that the mem- bers might well consider the desirability of increasing their annual subscriptions. Subscriptions are due on the Ist May, and members are earnestly asked to remit the same to the Secretary as early as possible after that to save a great amount of additional work The attention of exhibitors is called to the rule of remitting entry fees when the entry is made: It causes much unnecessary trouble when this is not,clon— it is with regret that the Council have received the resignation of the Assistant Secretary, Mr. J. S. Bate. who has always taken a keen interest in the work of the'society, and which he has so willingly assisted in promoting. The subscriptions this year are within ICls the same as last year, although a larger number of new members were elected this year than n any previous one. On the other hand 44 subscribers of last year from some cause or other did not renew their subscriptions, and their total subscriptions amounted to £3l Is. 6d. The total number'of members is now 39`.1, and their sub- scriptions amount to .£297 10s. 6d. Out of this number 293 pay subscriptions of 10s. and 10s. 6d. According to the rules of the Society the minimum subscription is 10s. All members of the society, irrespective of the amount of their subscription. the President has hitherto invited to his private lunch and they get an entrance . ticket to the show and have the same privileges as regards reduced entry fees as subscribers who give a larger amount. The Council therefore feel that the time has come when an appeal should be made for increased financial support, and all members who subscribe 10s. are asked to reconsider their subscrip- tions, to see if they cannot give such. an /amount as will materially increase the income of the Society, and so reduce the ever repeated deficit on the year's workings. The Council again advises the extension of the Society's district so far as a part of Cheshire is concerned. The Society is also asked to amend the existing rule as regards subscriptions and the right of entering exhibits at members' rates. Due notice of these two alterations in the rules appear on the notice of the annual meeting sent to all the members. The Council wish to thank the stewards of the Show and all officials who assisted so ably in carrying out the duties in connection with the 1900 Show. The reports were approved. ELECTION OF OFFICERS.</p>
        <p>Mr. C. E. THNAK ER, jun., proposed the re-election of Sir Gilbert Greenall as president of the society. In the past Sir Gilbert had done a great deal for the Society and he hoped that kindness would be extended to them in the future. (Applause.) Mr. H. F. GREENOUG H seconded and the proposal was heartily carried. Sir GrLuEuT in returning thanks said he.could assure the members he was only too pleased to act in the capacity of president and he would do everything he could in the fcture to enatire the success of the Society. (A oplause.) It was decided to invite Colonel Legh, High Legh, to,. accept the office of vice-president. In the event ol Colonel Legh being unable to do so it was decided to offer the vice-presidency to Lord Stanley. The following gentlemen were appointed the council :—Walter Bennett, Bruche ; H. Singleton. Warrington ; H. J. Turton, Hale ; T. M. West, Warrington : Jos. Shakeshaft, Lymm ; S. Gould, Lvmm ; Thos. Warburton, Daresbury ; and J.l Milbourne, Aston Heath. SOCIETY'S RADIUS INCREASED.</p>
        <p>The PRESIDENT, in moving the extension of the Society's rsdius to 16 miles from Market Gate on the Cheshire side, with the Stockport railway forming the Northern boundary, said the matter came before them last year but the members were not prepared to accept it. They had thonght it over during the year. and feel- ing that their Society, in certain classes, did not receive the amount of interest which it ought to have, the Council decided to advise the extension of the area fon the Cheshire side, so as to include a larger cheese and butter making area. Re thought the extension would also strengthen the light horse and hunter classes. (Applause). Mr. C. E. lANAKErt, jun., seconded. It was necessary to do something to increase the interest in the Society. Every year they had to look to the generosity of their ' President to make up their deficit, whereas they ought to he able to mike the Society go without that, If it were not for Sir Gilbert they would become bankrupt. • (Hear, bear, and applause.) Mr. A. J. WILLETT strongly supported, and the; motion was unanimously approved. SUBSCRIPTIONS.</p>
        <p>It was decided that in future new members of the Society must on joining pay the first three years' sub- scription in sdvanoe. It was explained that this was done because in the past large exhibitors had become members of the Society for one year in order to secure the reduced entranea fee, and had thereby saved rnoneY:i At the close Sir Gilbert Greenall was heartily then'' for presiding and for his interest in the Society.</p>
