        <p>/ plaiMng of overcrowding next. .It is getting too easy to obtain food and lodgings at his Majesty's expense. * * - Mr. Whitley, at a meeting of the Stockton Heath , Parochial Committee on Tuesday. said that some of 1 the back passages in the village were a disgrace to 1 civilization. We would be interested to hear Mr. , Whitley's opinion of certain streets in Warrington particularly Priory-street and Fletcher-street —which suffer by comparison with the worst that Stockton Heath can produce. 0 . 0 A new Siamese paper inake3 the following interest- announcement : ' The news of English, we tell the latest. Writ in perfectly style and most earliest. Do a murder get commit, we hear of and tell it. Do a mighty chief die, we publish it, and in borders of sombre. Staff has each one been colleged and write like Kipling and the Dickens. We circle very town and extortionate not for advertisements." * * * The Ship Canal Company has at last consented to the planting of trees and shrubs along the banks of the Ship Canal between the Cantilever Bridge at Latchford and the Stag Bridge at Walton. This affords a good oppertunity for removing the trees which iacommode the footpath on the north side of Fairfield- road to the other side of the rails. There is plenty of room on the Canal bank and none on the footpath. * * * Mr. A. J. Jolley is never so happy as when coin- ing some striking original phrase. On Monday he told the Education Committee that to allow fees to be charged in some schools and not in others was to " subsidise snobbery." The committee, however, did not share this view, and refused to sacrifice the income from parents who are willing to pay for some degree of social exclusion —which is not quite the same thing as " snobbery." * * * Free-speech has won the day in Widnes, and the Salvation Army or Labour Party may in future con- tinue their gatherings in Victoria-square secure from police interference. Altogether fifty Salvationists have been summoned for the alleged obstruction, and five have been sent to Walton Gaol. The Army brought up such reserves that the police found they were getting their hands more than full and retired from a conflict they ought to have had More sense than instigate.</p>
