        <p>Personal. Many of our readers will be pleased to learn that Mr. W. Hayes, Latchford, who recently underwent an operation for appendicitis is progressing very favour- ably towards recovery. Licensing Change. At the Borough Police Court on Monday, Mr. William Jones was granted temporary authority to sell at the off-licensed beer-house at 11, Brick-street, in lieu of Mr.°Lloyd Jones. LADIES' BEST GLACE BOOTS AND SHOES, UNDER THE CLOCK, BRIDGE-STREET.—Advt. Christmas Tree. The annual Christmas tree in connection with the I Latchford Baptist Church was on exhibition in the schoolroom on Monday evening. The proceeds were in aid of the Sunday school and arnounfed to over .9.10, Death of Mr. Thomas B. Potter. The death occurred early on Wedneday morning of Mr. Thomas B. Patter, licensee of the King's Arms, Buttermarket-street, and who for many years was in the employ of the Landon and North-Western Railway Company. Deceased who was 42 years of age had been ill for several days. PUBLIC BENEFIT, UNDER TILE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—AdVt. Christmas Hot-Pot His Worship the Mayor (Alderman Smethurst) will j give the usual hot-pot to the deserving poor on I Christmas Day. The distribution will take place at the Drill Hall from 11 a.m. to 12 o'clock. Tickets have been issued to the Clergymen of the town and to Mr. J. Fargher the Distress Committee's inquiry officer, to Mr. Cox, N.S.P.C.C., and applications if made to any of these gentlemen will be considered. Popular Penny Concerts. There was again a crowded attendance at the popular Penny concert in the Bold-street schools, on Saturday evening when an dkcellent programme, arranged by Mr. J. T. Barton was provided. The chair was occupied by Mr. C, E. Parker. Songs were given by Miss B. Eckersley, Mr. and Mrs. Hannan, and the two latter were warmly applauded for a duet. Master B. Barton pleased the audience with his violin solos. Mr. W. W. Whittle, who was announced to appear as the humorous, failed to put in an appear- ance, and his place was taken by Mr. Ashworth who was greatly appreciated. Mr. Pearce acted as accom- panist. UNDER THE CLOCK for Good BOOTS. Advt.— Silver-street Girls' Club. There was a large attendance of the members of the above club at a Christmas tea and entertainment which were held in the Silver-street School on Monday evening. After an excellent tea the president (Mrs. Garnett) spoke a few words to the members, then the following ladies kindly entertained them : —Mrs. Grifaiths. Miss Pierpoint, Miss Nerah Fairclough, Miss M. Fairclough. Mr. Moore supplied some fine gramophone selections and the girls sang hymns and I carols. Mrs. Bishop, Miss A. M. Dodds, and Miss IJackson also were present and assisted at the tea. The committee thank all -friends who contribute towards the support of the club, and who help in carrying on the work, esrecially the matron (Mrs. ITimmis), and the members of the Y Branch of the I B. W.T.A. XMAS PRESENTS in large variety, SPA windows.— J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge St., Warrington.— Advt. Funeral of the Late Mr T. Hesketh. The faneral of the late Mr. T. Hesketh took place in St. Paul's churchyard on Monday afternoon. In accordance with a wish frequently expressed by the deceased the ceremony was as quiet as possible. The funeral service was conducted by the Rev. W. Bracecamn. The mourners were Messrs. John Hesketh, Hesketh, of Barnsley, and Thomas Hesketh, (sons), Thomas Pennington (son-in-law), John Hesketh (nephew), and Ainsworth. By request no wreaths were forwarded. Mr. Hesketh, who was in his S9th year was a member of the well-known-- firm of .decoratofs. In his eatly days, he devoted a great deal of his spare time to organ building, in which he was remarkably clever, having produced many first class instruments. He was also a photographer of a high order, and was one of the first amateurs in Warrington. Oddfellowship. A successful social in connection with the "Prince of Wales Lodge" of Oddfellows was held at the Pelican Hotel on Saturday. N.G.Charles Mounfield presided, an d was supported by P.G.M. T. P. Bradshaw, L.G.M. H.-Maddock, P.P.G.M. Norbury, P.G .'s King. Webster, I Wadsworth, Marsh, Freeman and others. The Chair- man congratulated Bro. T. P. Bradshaw (the Grand Master of the Warrington district) on his appointment on the Education Comnaittee as representative of the friendly societies of Warrington. The N.G. read the actuaries report and valuation of the assets and liabitities which showed that the lodge was progressive in every way, and that a position "free from deficiency" would be attained at an early date. The proportion of assets tu liabilities in the SiCE and funeral fund VMS 99•1 per cent. (Applause.) Songs were well rendered by Brothers Tynan, Reddish, Freeman, Norbury and D. Whitham' and a recitation by P.P.G.11. J. Massey. P.G. Fred Webster acted as pianist</p>
